    14	
    15	He laid the book open on her desk, and showed her the place, and where it ended.
    16	
    17	"As it stands," he said. "Every stop."
    18	
    19	She began. He stood at her shoulder, as the manual said an officer was to stand when a minute left the records table, and read each line as she made it against the line it came from.
    20	
    21	He had not read it yet himself; he had only heard it, and he had always known that those were different things. He had sat forty feet from the middle chair with his pen moving, and had written a time, and had kept his eyes on his own page while a voice he had worked under for years said a thing slowly into an empty room. Now he read it.
    22	
    23	The second of the two returns he had by heart. He had read it once before, upside down, on a desk at Norhold, in perhaps three seconds, and had written it that night at the head of a sheet of his own, which lay in his kit at the inn, the fifth of six. He watched the copyist make the words, and they were the same words, and he felt nothing about them that he had not felt already.
    24	
    25	The first he had never seen. He had carried the file it answered; he had laid that file at her elbow in a hall on a bluff, square to the edge, and gone back to the foot of the table. A return had gone into it then, and he had not read it, because a records officer did not read what was addressed to the senior seat.
    26	
    27	*Determination: no originating authority of record.*
    28	
    29	The copyist's pen went across it without a check, and his eyes stayed on the line when her pen had gone on.
