    81	At the inn by the bridge he ate his supper downstairs among the carters and went up while it was still light.
    82	
    83	The river ran low and plain under the middle pier. He set the kit on the table by the window and unbuckled it, and took the six sheets from between the second manual and the third, and laid them out in their order, left to right, as he had laid them on the night of the seventh sitting. A mark with no office. Three between, each in its year. A sentence that shut a door. A seal on no register.
    84	
    85	He read them through, slowly, as an officer reads anything he means to rely on. Then he did something he had not done to them before. He took the pencil from the kit's loop and wrote a small figure in the top corner of each, from one to six. They had never been numbered, for to number a thing was to say how many of it there were to be.
    86	
    87	He did not take a clean sheet. He looked at the place where the clean sheets lay, and thought about what a seventh would say. It would say that on the fourth call day the senior seat read two returns into the minute; and that was in the minute, and pinned up in a porch. The six held what the registers could not answer, or would not, and this the registers had answered themselves, aloud. A thing that stood in the public record did not want a private sheet.
    88	
    89	He tied the six with a length of the red tape he used for receipts, once round and once across, and made the knot flat.
    90	
    91	Six entries. Done.
    92	
    93	It was a strange word to think, because nothing whatever was done. The mark stood on the file as it had always stood, and the seal he had hunted through three ledgers still belonged to no office. He had six facts and no leave to add them up, and he had never once tried.
    94	
    95	But his part of it was done. He had kept them until somebody whose office it was said one of them aloud.
    96	
