    98	
    99	It was a form. He had drawn it from the district seat's stationery press some weeks ago, on the day after the eleventh sitting, and had carried it blank ever since: *Request for relief from rotation*, a single leaf with five ruled heads. He had never filled one in. In all his service he had gone where he was sent and stayed until he was fetched.
   100	
   101	*Officer.* He wrote his name and grade.
   102	
   103	*Present duty.* He wrote it as his order had it: *Records, junior chair, institutional proceedings at Ostrand; the seat's Fifth-days, until relieved.*
   104	
   105	*Relief sought upon.* He considered that for some time, and wrote *The conclusion of the proceedings by ruling.* It was tomorrow, and a man might fairly ask to be relieved of a duty on the day the duty ended.
   106	
   107	*Posting sought.* The district seat's records room was a man short. He had seen it for himself when he went there with a rubbing of a seal: an impression book four months behind, and a ledger of dies that nobody had balanced against the cutters' copies since the last officer left. He wrote *District records, Ostrand, or any records room that is short.* He did not write *out of monitoring*, for the form had no line for what a man wished to leave.
   108	
   109	*Grounds.*
   110	
   111	The pen stayed over it.
   112	
   113	He knew the grounds. He had sat forty feet from a table for a year of Fifth-days and had not looked at it. He had looked at that boy before, on a pump step and at Greyvane and on a sprung floor at the bluff; he remembered every one of the looks, and had sworn off another, and had kept to it for a year by looking at his own hands. He was tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hours, and he wished to work in a room where the leaves were only leaves.
   114	
   115	None of that was grounds. He had never kept a sheet for what he felt, and a form was not the place to begin.
   116	
   117	*The proceedings conclude. The district records room stands a man short, to the officer's own knowledge, and its impression book is in arrear.*
   118	
   119	It was true, every word. He read it over once and signed it and dated it for the morning, and laid it on top of the kit, where he would see it when he woke, to hand in at the district seat on his way to the exchange.
   120	
   121	The bridge-ward had lit his lantern. Havel sat at the window with his aching hand open on his knee and watched it, and thought, a little, about the young man at the far table, who had sat up straight for a year. He did not know what a ruling would do to him. He had never known what any of it would do; that had not been his to know. He found, sitting there, that he hoped the registers would be kind to him. He knew of no register that entered kindness, and he hoped it all the same.
   122	
   123	Then the lantern went out, and he went to bed.
   124	
   125	---
   126	
   127	"I can see both doors," said Bracken, "exactly as I said."
   128	
   129	They were at the head of the market street, the two of them, at the second bell on the last Fifth-day, where the cobbles began to fall toward the river. Below on the left were the four steps of the wool exchange, with people gathering on them already for a ruling that was hours off. Along a side street to the right, three corners down, a blue door stood in a plain stone front with a board beside it, and Brom and Lira were walking toward it.
   130	
   131	Cael watched them go, because he could not go with them. He was a respondent on a call day, and though the district hall was well within the bell, a respondent did not walk into another proceeding's room on the morning of his own ruling; the house's counsel had said so, and then had said that she was sorry. So he stood with the registrar and saw a broad back in a good coat and a narrow one beside it go down a street. Brom walked as he walked to a frame, and Lira had her hands in her pockets and was, for once, not talking.
   132	
   133	"She said nobody goes in alone," said Cael.
   134	
   135	"She said it to me at breakfast, as though I had proposed the opposite." Bracken had the calendar card in his hand, the stiff one, and was turning it over and over without reading it. "Eight," he said. "That will be the eighth advancement docket I have seen go down from the hill. Seven have been found for the tier. I have no reason to think the district has changed its habits."
   136	
   137	The blue door opened, and the two backs went in, and it shut.
   138	
   139	It was the longest hour of the morning. He spent it at the trough by the exchange's corner, counting the people on the steps as they came, and stopped at ninety. Bracken went in to see the seat's clerk about the house's copy and came out again, and the older coat stood across the street under an awning with his stick.
   140	
   141	Then Lira came round the corner at something that was nearly a run, and stopped herself, and walked, and Brom came after her at his own pace. He looked just as he had looked going in, with his coat buttoned, except that there were two red bars across his forearms where the cuffs had ridden up, darker than the wool press had left them.
   142	
   143	"They loaded it," he said.
   144	
   145	"And?"
   146	
   147	"I held it." He thought about how to say the rest. "It was more than Ephram ever put on, a good deal over. They let it down on springs, and then they let it down again while I had it, which he never did, and I heard the middle one say *forty* and go on counting." He looked at his forearms. "The plate lit. I felt it under my boots. It's warm. I didn't know they were warm."
   148	
   149	"What did it say?" said Cael.
   150	
   151	"They don't tell you. They write. It goes to the registrar by the bag." Brom glanced at Bracken, and Bracken inclined his head. "Three of them at a table, and a clerk, and a frame the size of a hay wagon. Nobody said a word to me but *stand there* and *thank you*."
   152	
   153	"It said Iron," said Lira. She had got her breath. "I was on the bench by the door and I watched the middle assessor's face when the plate came up. He looked like a man who'd put his hand in his pocket for a coin and found the coin he expected." She shoved her hands deeper into her own pockets. "He was right about himself, in front of people whose job it was to check. I saw it happen. I wanted somebody to have seen it who knew what it was."
   154	
   155	Brom did not say anything to that. He stood in the market street with his forearms marked and his face quite calm, and Cael saw that it had been enough. Whatever a clerk wrote on whatever paper, a plate had come up warm under his boots and said what was so.
   156	
   157	"The hour after noon," said Bracken, and put the card away. "I suggest that everybody eats."
   158	
   159	---
   160	
   161	The long room held more than it had ever held, and nobody in it made a sound.
   162	
   163	They were standing along the walls, and the doors had been left open so that the steps could hear, and the steps were full, and past them the street. Everybody who had come down for the closing had come again, and Ephram's cohort had the ninth and tenth benches, and this time nobody had needed to threaten them.
   164	
   165	Cael sat behind Withrow's chair with lot seventeen under his boot, and he sat up straight. It was, as the counsel had told him once, nearly the last time he would have to.
   166	
   167	The five chairs were filled. At the records table the junior chair had his pen ready, and Jent sat with his hands folded on a shut folder and his spectacles hanging.
   168	
   169	"The seat," said the clerk.
   170	
   171	It came in and sat, and it had a single leaf in front of it.
   172	
   173	"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. For ruling. The fourteenth sitting."
   174	
   175	The hood read. It read in the voice it had used all year, level and unhurried, with no more weight on one word than on another, and it paused at the end of each sentence so that the clerk might have it. Cael fixed his eyes on the exchange's clock over the doors.
   176	
   177	"*The seat, having heard the petition and the responses in full, and having found the enrollment lawful in its execution and the respondent institution compliant with its charter throughout — which findings stand undisturbed — nonetheless finds the evaluation-integrity question sustained on the merits. The demonstration provision describes the practitioner; it does not evaluate him within the meaning of the integrity provisions. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol is granted. The re-evaluation shall be conducted at the district Arbiter station within five weeks. Should the re-evaluation return no valid classification, the procedure of the code's chapter fourteen shall apply: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. The demonstration-provision enrollment is revoked effective at the close of the present academic year.*
   178	
   179	"*Given under the Compact's hand.*"
   180	
   181	Two minutes and forty seconds.
   182	
   183	"So ruled," said the hood.
   184	
   185	"So ruled," said the clerk, and wrote, and at the records table the junior chair wrote the time.
   186	
   187	Cael had thought, for a year, that when it came he would feel the floor go, and he had braced for that as he braced for a landing. He sat with his hands flat on the house's table and waited for it, and the lot number stayed under his boot, and the boards stayed where they were.
   188	
   189	Lawful. Compliant. Revoked.
   190	
   191	It was all one paragraph, and the first two words were given away in a clause, as findings that *stand undisturbed*, before the *nonetheless*. He had heard that word coming since the preliminary, when the seat had said *sound* and *whole* and *notwithstanding* in one breath. Karis's fifty-one minutes had been answered in a single sentence: the provision described him, and did not evaluate him. The counsel had asked a table on the first night whether they knew the difference, in front of a seat, between a description and an evaluation, and here it was, a year later, in the seat's own mouth.
   192	
   193	There was no trick in it. A year of Fifth-days had been walking toward exactly this leaf, and every door on the way had been real law and had opened one way, as Karis had told them on the first night. He had read it better than they did, and so had she, and it had made no difference, because it had never turned on the reading.
   194	
   195	What he felt was not the floor going. It was a corridor ending. He had been walking down it so long that he had stopped feeling the walls, and now there was a last door, with a year's close chalked on it, and behind him nothing whatever that he needed to go back for.
   196	
   197	The seat rose. It gathered its one leaf, and turned, and went to its own door, and for a moment the grey back was all there was of it. He had tried at fourteen sittings to learn something from how that shape sat, and had wanted a face under it, because a face could be read, and hated, and found out.
   198	
   199	*Presiding seat per seal.* He had copied the words off a notice in the year's fourth week, and today he believed them. There was somebody under the cloth, and whoever it was would go home, and hang the hood on a peg, and tell the truth about the day. But it was not that person who had ruled. A seal had ruled. That was what the hood was for, and it was not a cruelty or even a disguise; it only showed, as plainly as cloth could, what he had been arguing with.
   200	
   201	The door shut behind it, and two hundred people breathed out.
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	Jent came across the aisle before the benches had finished rising.
   206	
   207	He came to Withrow first. He stood in front of her, a man of middle height in a coat with mended cuffs, and bowed, not to the registry's depth but a little past it.
   208	
   209	"Chancellor," he said. "Your house is clear in every line of that leaf. I asked that it should be, in terms, and I wanted you to hear so from the petitioner and not from a clerk. If an inspector ever opens your folder at this year, send him to the finding that the respondent institution was compliant throughout."
   210	
   211	"I heard it read," said Withrow. Her voice was perfectly even. "I shall know where to send him."
   212	
   213	He turned to Karis, and for once he seemed to have nothing ready. He looked at her for a moment over the shut wallet.
   214	
   215	"Advocate," he said, and that was all. He said it as he had said it into the minute at the fourth sitting, when it had been a courtesy; and it was not one now.
   216	
   217	Then he looked at Cael, and Cael looked back and did not send the read. He had sat across from this man at a conference table in the year's fourth week and found nobody in the coat, and he knew why now. There had been nobody to find. Jent had been sent with an honest question, and had asked it honestly, and had been given the answer the code gave.
   218	
   219	"Hesk-ward," said Jent. He nodded, the same nod he had given on a step in the rain. He seemed about to say something more, and to think better of it, and he picked up his one folder and his inkwell and went out by the side door with his thin clerk behind him. He had another docket somewhere, and he did not look back at the room.
   220	
   221	In the stable yard Withrow stood by her carriage with one gloved hand on its door, and waited until Karis and Cael had come up, and Bracken with the seal.
   222	
   223	"I want one thing said before the hill gets hold of it," she said. "I read losing filings for twenty years at the registry. It was my work to say why they had lost. They lost because a date was wrong, or a section was misread, or somebody had hoped a seat would not notice a thing it was paid to notice." She looked at Karis, and then, longer, at Cael. "You did not lose badly. I should like that to be the first thing either of you remembers about today, and I shall say it to anybody who asks me. There is not a line in that ruling that answers a line of yours. It could not answer them. It went round."
   224	
   225	"It still went against us," said Karis.
   226	
   227	"It did. You lost to a discretion, and nobody has ever beaten one of those from inside it. I knew that on the eighth day." Withrow opened the carriage door herself. "I chose to stand there anyway, and I'd choose it again, and now I can show anybody who asks me exactly how well it was done. That is not nothing. It is a good deal of what I have."
   228	
   229	She got in. For a moment, before the door shut, he saw her face with nobody looking at it, and it was the face of a woman who has known a thing for four days and is not going to say it in a stable yard. He did not understand that then, and put it away to look at later.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	Bracken changed the slate before he took his coat off.
   234	
   235	He rubbed out the three lines with the heel of his hand, the two closings and the ruling, and left the fourth, because that was Brom's and not the house's. He wrote in their place, square and plain:
   236	
   237	*Ruling given. No finding against the house. The enrollment revoked, from the year's close.*
   238	
   239	He read it over, and dusted his fingers, and went behind his counter, where the lamps were lit in pairs down the length of the records hall and both his clerks were already bent over the middle table.
   240	
   241	"Copy-sets," he said, to Cael, who had not asked. "The Chancellor's order, from the carriage. Thirty-four houses wrote to ask what this one would do if the seat went against it. They are to have the ruling in the house's own fair copy by tomorrow's first bag, with the standing entries, and no gloss. Three leaves to a set, and five sets more for the faculties." He pulled down his second glasses. "Thirty-nine. I have told the kitchen to send bread."
   242	
   243	Seln was at the counter's far end. He had come down in the coach's corner without a word, and he stood now under the counter lamp with the enrollee's copy of the ruling laid in front of him on the wood. It was the seat's own engrossment, on registry stock, with the wax of the river and the scale at its foot; Cael had carried it up the hill in the satchel and had not been able to think what to do with it.
   244	
   245	Seln did not read it, for he had heard it read. He held it up to the lamp by two corners and looked through it, for some time. Then he laid it flat and took Bracken's boxwood rule from its nail, and measured something at the sheet's edge, and measured it again from the other side. He turned the leaf over and ran his thumbnail very lightly round the rim of the seal, as Bracken had once done under an arch, and bent close to the last line to look at the hand.
   246	
   247	"Well?" said Cael.
   248	
   249	Seln laid the rule down.
   250	
   251	"I have read paper all year for what stood behind it," he said. "A mark a sixteenth low. A lot written above the ruled line, with a mark for a word. A seal that is on no schedule. I have been reading paper that way for a good while longer than a year." He touched the leaf's corner. "This is registry stock from the first mould, and the mark sits where it should. The die has its number in the rim, and the number will be on its line in the cutters' ledger; I should be surprised if it were not. The hand is the seat's own clerk's. I have watched him write it since the fortieth day."
   252	
   253	"So it's honest."
   254	
   255	"It is exactly what it says it is. There is nothing behind it at all." He said it in his mild voice, as he might have reported a floor schedule. "In my trade a man is taught four grades of pressure, in their order. The record. The instrument. The knock, which is paper nobody will own. And last the hand, which is a road with no paper at all. I was taught that the hand was the worst of them, and I believed it for fifteen years." He shook his head, very slightly. "I had the order wrong. What stood in the road was a glove." He looked down at the last line of the leaf. "*Given under the Compact's hand.* That is the only true sentence of its kind I have been shown this year. This is the hand. It does not have another."
   256	
   257	He squared the leaf to the counter's edge, and stepped back from it.
   258	
   259	"I am sorry," he said. "I should have liked to find something. It would have been easier for you."
   260	
   261	Cael looked at the engrossment under the lamp.
   262	
   263	*I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.* That was his own line, from the year's sixth week, and he had gone on looking, at Jent and at a hood and down a road with chalk on it. He stopped looking. It was like setting down a thing he had carried so long that he had forgotten it had weight.
   264	
   265	At the middle table the copy-sets were coming off the clerks' pens, and Brom was squaring them.
   266	
   267	Nobody had asked him to. He had come in behind them and stood about for a little with his coat on, a man who had held a frame that morning and had nothing to do with his arms. Then he had gone to the table's end, where the finished leaves were being laid in threes, and had taken up each three as it came, and knocked its edges true on the wood, long side and short side, and laid it on the pile with its corners exactly over the corners beneath.
   268	
   269	Then he began to weigh them. He did it on his palm; he lifted each set a little, and let it settle, and put it down. Cael watched him do nine, and on the tenth his hand stayed up.
   270	
   271	"This one's light," said Brom.
   272	
   273	The senior clerk looked round. "It's three leaves."
   274	
   275	"It's light."
   276	
   277	The clerk took it, and counted, and counted again more slowly with a wet thumb. There were two; the standing entries had been left out of it. He went red and reached for a clean sheet, and Brom took up the next.
   278	
   279	He had said, in a harness room, that he could not read a registry sheet anymore; that since the case he looked at one and saw only what it was a form of. He was not reading these. He was doing the one honest thing he could still do with such paper, which was to say what it weighed.
   280	
   281	Lira was on the stair. It was the narrow stair behind the counter, the one that went up to Bracken's rooms, and she sat on its fourth step with her knees drawn up and her chin on them, in the shadow above the lamps. She had not said a word since the coach, and she was watching Brom's hands.
   282	
   283	She did not look like somebody who had lost. He knew that face; she had worn it on the north wall when she first asked why they were still there, and on the night after a dairy. She was adding something up.
   284	
   285	He left the engrossment where Seln had squared it, and went to find Karis. It was nearly the tenth bell.

