     1	# Chapter 48 — Two Hundred and Six
     2	
     3	The long room was full, and he counted it.
     4	
     5	It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
     6	
     7	So he sat with lot seventeen under his left boot and his hands flat on the house's table, and counted the people, as he had once counted the words in an instrument.
     8	
     9	He took the benches first. There were forty of them, and for eleven sittings he had been able to count the heads on them at a glance. Today he had to go bench by bench, turning a little in his chair, and Bracken, beside him, watched him do it and said nothing, having very probably done it himself on the way in. Twenty-three had folding boards on their knees. There was a whole bench of district clerks in their grey, who had come because it was their seat and their Fifth-day. There were wool-factors, who owned the room and seemed to feel that it had been borrowed for long enough. An instructor in a blue-grey coat sat near the back with her arms folded, and he thought she was the lake house's. On the fourth bench sat Lira, Brom, Gault and the assistant with her inky collar, and at its far end, by the pillar, where a man could see the whole platform and both tables without turning his head, Seln sat with his coat buttoned.
    10	
    11	A hundred and eighty-seven on the benches.
    12	
    13	Then the people who were there by office. The seat's clerk at his small table. Ilsev in the middle chair below the platform, with her file at its angle, and her four district evaluators. The senior chair and the junior at the records table. At the petitioner's table Jent, and for the first time a clerk of his own, a thin young man with a satchel who looked as if he had been told not to touch anything. Three officers at the doors. At the house's table Withrow, the counsel, Karis, Bracken with the seal on his knees, and himself.
    14	
    15	And the hood, when it came.
    16	
    17	Nineteen. He added them twice, which was once more than the sum needed.
    18	
    19	Two hundred and six.
    20	
    21	He did not stop there, though he might have. He went down them again, more slowly, and this time he was not counting heads. He was doing what he had done on the night the instrument was served, when he had gone through nineteen leaves looking for the place where it lied. He had looked for that place in every sitting since. He had looked for it in Jent's weight in a chair, and in a seal that Havel had found on no register, and under a grey hood that had never once shown him a face.
    22	
    23	The clerks in their grey had come to see their own seat do its work. The correspondents would write down what was said, and get some of it wrong, and mean none of the errors. The four evaluators were working officers who had read every exhibit they were handed. Ilsev had asked the seat to minute the edge of her own chair, so that nobody could push her past it. At the records table sat a man who had receipted a hundred and seventy-nine leaves without losing one. And Jent, with his travelling inkwell and his mended cuffs, had given away every point that was not true as soon as he was shown it.
    24	
    25	There was nobody. He had known it for weeks, in the way he knew a figure he had not yet written down, and this morning, with the room full, he wrote it down.
    26	
    27	Two hundred and six people, and not one of them was dishonest.
    28	
    29	He did not know what he had expected a thing like this to look like. He had thought, when he was younger, that it would have a face somewhere, and that the face would look away. He looked along the benches at a hall of people who would all go home tonight and tell the truth about their day.
    30	
    31	"The seat," said the clerk, and two hundred and four of them stood up, and Cael stood with them.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the close. The twelfth sitting. The petitioner."
    36	
    37	Jent rose.
    38	
    39	He had no folder today. He had left even the one folder on the table, shut, with his spectacles lying on it, and he stood in front of the platform with his hands at his sides, as a man stands who has come to say a thing he could say in his sleep and does not wish to seem to be reading it.
    40	
    41	"The seat allowed each party an hour," he said. "The petitioner will use ten minutes, and I should like to say why, so that it is not taken for discourtesy. There is very little left between the parties. I have spent a year making sure of that."
    42	
    43	He turned his head a little toward the house's table.
    44	
    45	"The enrollment was lawfully made. The provision under which it was made is law, and has not been repealed, and the seat has held so. The renewals are good upon their days; the respondent's table of them is the petitioner's own. The evaluator of record is competent, and his figures are honest, and his notes were produced entire. The tournament office's record is accepted. And the house has kept within its charter in every particular I have been able to examine, and I examined a great many." He paused. "I want one of those in the minute in my own voice, and not only in a schedule. The Chancellor has done nothing wrong. She answered under escort. She read every order at her own gate. She produced what was asked before it fell due. If every respondent I met were the Chancellor, I should have very little work, and I should think the better of the registry for it."
    46	
    47	Withrow did not move. Cael, behind her chair, saw the line of her grey collar, and it was as straight as it had been at the first sitting.
    48	
    49	"That is what is agreed," said Jent. "Here is what is asked, and it will take me four sentences. A practitioner is enrolled in a chartered house. The standard protocol has not classified him. The registry asks that he be evaluated under that protocol, as every practitioner in every chartered house is evaluated. If the protocol reads him, his enrollment stands, and I shall be the first man in this room to say so."
    50	
    51	He let that lie a moment.
    52	
    53	"If it does not, the code says what follows. It said so before anybody here was born. The petitioner did not write the fourteenth chapter and does not ask the seat to add a word to it. He asks only whether the registry may put the question. That is a matter of discretion, and I have said so since the fourth sitting, in answer to the best question I was asked all year."
    54	
    55	He inclined his head an inch toward Karis, and she inclined hers the same inch back. They might have been two people who had met on a narrow stair every week for a year.
    56	
    57	"It may," said Jent. "The seat held at the preliminary that the question was cognizable. Nothing the respondent has filed since has touched that holding, and nothing was meant to; her filings have been upon the facts, and the facts were hers before she filed. So I have nothing to answer. I have used ten minutes because the seat was generous with the hour, and I did not like to seem ungrateful."
    58	
    59	He sat down, and screwed the lid of his inkwell a quarter turn tighter, though it had not been open.
    60	
    61	Cael had sent the read after that man five times in the year and found nobody in the coat. He did not send it now, and he found that he no longer wanted to. He had spent months wishing Jent would turn out to be the arm, because an arm could be found out. But Jent was a hand, and a clean one. He had said exactly what he was asking for, in four sentences, before two hundred people, and every one of the four was true.
    62	
    63	That was the edge on him. Cael saw it plainly for the first time, sitting behind Withrow's chair. A liar would have had to keep his story straight for fourteen sittings, and somewhere in a hundred and seventy-nine exhibits Karis would have caught him. An honest man had only to stand up and ask.
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	The house's counsel took twelve minutes, and she spent them all on one thing.
    68	
    69	She did not go back over the year. She laid a single sheet on the edge of the table, with her steel rule across it, and told the seat what the house had found when it went looking for a like case, and how far back it had gone, and in whose digests.
    70	
    71	"The registry has ordered frames tested," she said. "It has ordered stations inspected, and records opened, and officers examined upon their conduct. It has ordered practitioners evaluated, a great many of them, where a provision had lapsed, or was wanting, or had been got by a false paper. The house has read every such order it could find." She turned the rule a quarter turn. "It has not found one in which a seat ordered a practitioner into a frame over a provision that stood. Not one. The petitioner was asked whether he had, at the ninth sitting. He answered that he had not, and that he did not need one."
    72	
    73	She was a very small woman, and the room had gone quiet for her as it always did.
    74	
    75	"He is right that he does not need one. A discretion needs no precedent. But the seat may like to know, before it uses a tool, that nobody has ever picked it up by that end." The rule ticked again. "That is the house upon the remedy. Upon the merits, the house's close is the work of its advocate of record, who drew every submission this seat has received from it. The house renews its motion under the second paragraph of the hearing provision, and asks that she be heard."
    76	
    77	She sat.
    78	
    79	Cael did not look at Karis, but he felt her beside him as he would have felt a lamp. She had her two hands laid flat on a shut wallet, pale fingers and all, and she had not written a word since the clerk spoke. She had thirty slips in that wallet. She had written every one of them out in full without knowing whether anybody would let her stand up.
    80	
    81	Under the hood there was the small stillness that came before it spoke.
    82	
    83	"The motion was laid before the seat at the eleventh sitting," said the hood. "It has had it under consideration since. The provision stands. Leave is given."
    84	
    85	Somebody on the benches let out a breath.
    86	
    87	"The advocate of record for the respondent will be heard at the next sitting. She will be heard once, and within the hour. The petitioner may reply, if he is so advised, and not otherwise. The seat will then reserve." The level voice went on with no more weight on it than before. "So ordered."
