   143	And it could not be said, in this room or in any room that stood on it. She had worked that out herself, over five nights, and had been the one to tell them. So she turned it face down. She did it where she stood, in front of two hundred people, with her thumb, and nobody saw.
   144	
   145	He had been sorry all year that she would have to argue as if the room were ground. He had not understood until now that she would also have to stand in it and leave out the best thing she knew, and that nothing in her face would say that it weighed anything. It was like watching Brom hold a bar.
   146	
   147	---
   148	
   149	"I have nearly done," said Karis.
   150	
   151	She told them what the house had made. It was not a boast, and she did not dress it; it was two years of a practitioner's work set down by the people whose office it was to set it down. This year alone there were seventeen panel notes, produced entire, and the leaf nobody had asked for was among them. There were a hundred and seventy-nine exhibits, and not one had been found out of its order; the records table had counted them, she was told, and she was content to rest on the records table's count. At that table the junior chair's pen stopped for the length of a word and went on.
   152	
   153	"The petitioner called it the best-documented enrollment he had reviewed in thirty years, and I believe him. If the remedy is granted, the registry will strike that record. In its place it will enter three words: *no valid classification.* It will exchange the fullest true entry in its books for an empty one, and it will do that under sections whose only purpose is that entries be true."
   154	
   155	She looked along the five chairs, and then up.
   156	
   157	"I do not say that would be unlawful. I say it is the one use of this discretion that defeats what the discretion is for."
   158	
   159	Then she spoke of the people who would come after, and she did it quickly, for she had cut it twice. A ruling stood in the registry's books, she said, for every house that held a charter in the same words. The seat had before it four old challenges to the clause, and in one of them a ferryman's daughter in a southern river house had waited a year for a station to settle upon what she was; the house had waited with her, and at the end of the year the station had read her, and she had stayed. Under the ruling now asked for, nobody need have waited. Somebody need only have filed.
   160	
   161	"The next person these sections reach will not have a chancellor who answers under escort," said Karis. "She will not have a registrar who reads every leaf, or an evaluator who hands up the note nobody wanted. She will not have an hour. I have had all of those, and I know what they are worth, and I know what it cost the people who gave them."
   162	
   163	She stood quite still.
   164	
   165	"The house asks for one thing. It does not ask the seat to hold the petition unlawful. It asks the seat to use the discretion it has for the purpose that is written on it. The entry is true. Twenty-two of the registry's own officers made it. Leave it standing." Her voice did not break, though he had been afraid that it would. "That is the whole of the respondent's close."
   166	
   167	She went back round the end of the table and sat down.
   168	
   169	Cael looked up at the exchange's clock over the doors, because he had promised to.
   170	
   171	Fifty-one minutes. She had dropped nine more slips on her feet, in front of the hood, without a mark on her face to show where, and when he went back over it he found them: every place where she would have told the seat a thing the other side had already signed. *I dislike sitting through an argument whose answer I already hold*, Jent had said on a step, and she had heard him. She had given a registry seat fifty-one minutes with nothing in them that anybody in the room already owned.
   172	
   173	Nobody made a sound. Ephram had told his cohort that a seat fined for cheering, and the ninth and tenth benches sat like two rows of posts.
   174	
   175	"The petitioner," said the hood. "If he is so advised."
   176	
   177	Jent stood. He took off his spectacles, and let them fall on their cord, and stood for a moment with his hands at his sides.
   178	
   179	"The registry does not dispute a syllable of it," he said.
   180	
   181	He said it to the platform, and then he turned and said the rest to her.
   182	
   183	"I closed a week ago, and I will not close twice. I rise only so that the advocate is not left to wonder whether she was heard. She was. Every proposition she has put to the seat is true. The petitioner adopts her account of what is asked, in her own words, the scale included; I could not have stated my own petition so exactly." He paused. "And it stands where it stood. She has shown the seat that the discretion would be unwisely used, and I think she has shown it beyond answer. But a discretion is not defeated by being shown to be unwise. It is only exercised, or it is not, and that is the seat's and never was mine."
   184	
   185	He sat down.
   186	
   187	It was the best that could have been said, and it was the worst, for he had not left her so much as a quarrel to go home with. Cael looked at the side of Karis's face, and it was perfectly composed, and her hands lay flat on the shut wallet.
   188	
   189	"The seat has heard the parties," said the hood. "Before it reserves, the clerk will read the standing entries."
   190	
   191	The clerk rose with the minute book.
   192	
   193	It was the dullest reading of the year, and it was meant to be: the count of a proceeding, set down so that a ruling could stand on it. Thirteen sittings held, upon their days. Exhibits of the petitioner, nine, received. Exhibits of the respondent, a hundred and seventy-nine, received, with the supplemental submissions and one correction slip. Orders of production, two, complied with. Statements entered under the provision for such statements, one, noted and set aside. The evaluation seat constituted at the third sitting, its scope as minuted.
   194	
   195	The four district evaluators were writing it down. They would have returns of their own to make, and they copied each line as it came, four pens moving together along the row.
   196	
   197	The clerk turned a leaf, and there was a form pasted to the next one. Cael could see the edge of it from where he sat, a single sheet of registry stock under the seal with the river and the scale. He knew the hand that had written on its ruled line, though he had never seen the hand. He had seen the man; he had sat across a cold stove from him, four streets back from this room, and heard him say what he would write.
   198	
   199	The clerk read the entry as he had read the others, in the code's own old words, as the form's column was headed.
   200	
   201	"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
   202	
   203	Four pens stopped.
   204	
   205	It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
   206	
   207	The evaluator nearest the records table looked at Ilsev. Ilsev did not look at him. She was looking at the clerk, with her file shut on her knee, and her face told Cael nothing whatever.
   208	
   209	On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. He sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
   210	
   211	"So entered," said the hood, and the pens began again.
   212	
   213	That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
   214	
   215	Cael found that it was enough, and it surprised him. He had wanted a wall, a month ago, and had been given a blank on a form. But he had watched Gault hand up a leaf because it was there, and he had heard Karis spend fifty-one minutes on a room that could not be moved, and he thought he was beginning to learn how the honest ones did it. They did not do it to win. They did the thing that was theirs to do, to its full weight, and let it be entered.
   216	
   217	"The seat reserves," said the hood. "It will give its ruling upon the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the hour after noon. From the First-day of that week the parties will keep within the bell, at the seat's call and the evaluation seat's. So ordered."
   218	
   219	"So ordered."
   220	
   221	The seat rose, and went out by its own door.
   222	
   223	---
   224	
   225	The house's counsel caught Karis on the second step, by the sleeve, which Cael had never seen her do to anybody.
   226	
   227	The crowd was coming out past them on both sides, quietly, like people leaving a place of worship. Rooke had gone down through it already with his board under his arm, and had touched two fingers to the board's edge as he passed Karis, which from Rooke was a speech.
   228	
   229	"Twenty-six years," said the counsel. She was looking up, with her small grey eyes very bright, and she still had hold of the sleeve. "Twenty-six years in front of seats, and I have heard perhaps four closings I should have been glad to sign. That is the best argument I have ever heard made in one of those rooms, and I include every one of mine. I want you to have that from somebody who is paid to know." She let go. "It will not have moved him. You knew that. You did it as though you didn't, and that is the whole of the trade."
   230	
   231	"He agreed with it," said Karis. Her voice had gone back to its usual size. "All of it. He stood up and agreed."
   232	
   233	"He did. I have never been so thoroughly beaten by a man agreeing with me, and neither have you." The counsel picked up her bag. "Go home. Eat."
   234	
   235	In the coach Brom unbuttoned his right-hand pocket and held out an apple, and Karis took it and ate it in about nine bites, core and all but the stalk, and then he held out the second and she ate that. Then she said, to nobody, "I left out the tournament charter's provisions. I meant to. I had a note for the cold term," and she put her head against the leather beside the window and was asleep before the bridge.
   236	
   237	She did not cry, though he had wondered whether she would. She slept with her mouth a little open and the wallet sliding off her knees, and Brom put out one hand and held it there, flat, all the way up the switchbacks.
   238	
   239	Lira sat opposite, looking at her.
   240	
   241	"She was better than him," she said, very low. "She was better than everybody. And it's going to go against us anyway, isn't it?"
   242	
   243	"Yes," said Cael.
   244	
   245	"Then what was it for?"
   246	
   247	He thought about it while the coach climbed. He had asked himself the same question in a reading room in the recess, in harder words than hers, and Karis had answered that she would write it as if it were ground and pay what that cost. He looked for the anger he had felt that day, and it was not there.
   248	
   249	"It wasn't for the seat," he said. "I thought it was, all year. It's in the minute now. Every word she said is there under her own name, in the registry's own presses, for as long as there's a registry, and somebody will read it. Some girl in a reading room with ink on her collar, looking for something else." He watched Karis's pale fingers, open on her knee. "And it was for her. She wanted to do the thing properly, once, with everybody looking. She's done it."
   250	
   251	Lira said nothing for a while.
   252	
   253	"I'd have cried," she said at last. "I nearly did, and I wasn't even the one saying it."
   254	
   255	"I know. I saw you sit on your hands."
   256	
   257	Bracken, in the corner, had the seal on his knees and his eyes shut.
   258	
   259	"Fourteen," he said. "One to come."

