    30	
    31	It was the steel rule.
    32	
    33	The counsel sat at the table's end with the code open in front of her, the fat volume with the schedules, and a single sheet beside it that was not the house's paper. Withrow sat across from her with both hands on the wood. Bracken stood by the hearth with his back to the room. His spectacles were pushed up on his forehead and he held a slip of paper in his hand, and he looked like a man who had read something off a wall and wished he had not been the one to walk past it.
    34	
    35	The counsel was speaking. Cael could not hear a word. He saw her lay one finger on a line in the code and then on the sheet, and then on the code again, lower down, as a person does who is adding days. He saw the rule turn its quarter turn at the end of it.
    36	
    37	Withrow said something short, and the counsel answered, and then for a long moment nobody in that room spoke at all.
    38	
    39	Then Withrow reached across the table and shut the code herself, with one hand, quite gently. She drew the single sheet toward her and folded it once and put it inside her coat. She looked up as she did it, and saw him through the pane.
    40	
    41	Her face did nothing. It was the registry face, the one she had worn at her own gate under four riders, and it rested on him for the space of a breath and gave him no more than a shut door would have. Then she turned her head and said something to Bracken, and Bracken pulled his spectacles down.
    42	
    43	Cael took his candle and went up.
    44	
    45	He told himself it was the ruling they were preparing for, some form to be ready, some order of who should stand where. It was very likely that. But he lay awake a while with Brom breathing across the room like a slow bellows, and he kept seeing her hand come across and close the book. It was the way a person shuts a ledger after reading the sum at the foot of it.
    46	
