    20	
    21	"Then you should have the whole of it," said Withrow, "since you have brought me yours." She took a folded sheet from inside her coat and opened it on the blotter and turned it toward him. It was not the house's paper. There were four lines on it in the small capitals of the house's counsel, and each had a day written against it.
    22	
    23	*Ruling: the Fifth-day.*
    24	
    25	*Notice of date, to the Chancellor, by a district officer: upon the ruling, or the day after.*
    26	
    27	*Evaluation: the Fourth-day following. The last day of the academic year.*
    28	
    29	*Revocation takes effect: at that day's close.*
    30	
    31	"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
    32	
    33	"So it isn't five weeks."
    34	
