   285	He had his board under his arm. He had come from the east hall, by the smell of chalk on him, and he stood in the gate with his feet apart and looked at the cart as he looked at a floor he had not yet charted. He looked at the gelding's white foot, and for some time at the bound arm.
   286	
   287	"Higher," he said to Cael. "Till it stops beating."
   288	
   289	Cael raised it.
   290	
   291	Rooke looked at Lira, and she looked back, and neither of them said anything at all, since it had been said at dawn, over a book. Then he looked at Brom, who was at the tailboard with the last of the rope in his hands. Brom had stood in a district's frame two days ago and felt a plate come up warm, and had left the paper for it under a film of dust; and Rooke knew that, for he had heard everything on the hill by the second bell of any morning, and had once had a meet record kept under a hall's seal for a fighter because paper could not be rubbed off.
   292	
   293	"Doors open both ways," said Rooke.
   294	
   295	Brom's hands stopped on the rope.
   296	
   297	He did not ask what it meant, and Cael, watching him, thought that he did not need to. A man had told him at a counter that morning that he ought to hear the hinge before he shut a door, and here was another, a coach, telling him in four words that a hinge worked in both directions. Rooke had looked at a large fighter roping somebody else's boxes, and he had priced it, as he priced everything.
   298	
   299	"I'll remember it," said Brom.
   300	
   301	"See you do." Rooke shifted the board under his arm. He nodded once, to the four of them and the horse together, and went back in, and they heard him shout at somebody in the court about a rope left on wet flags.
   302	
   303	Lira took the stones from behind the wheels.
   304	
   305	---
   306	
   307	They went down the bluff road at dusk.
   308	
   309	It was six switchbacks. He had climbed them first with a provision and a stamp, and he had gone down them all this year in a coach, on the seat's Fifth-days, sitting up straight; and he walked them now beside a cart's wheel, with his hip complaining at each turn, where it went downhill, and his shoulder beginning to tell him about six pushes. He let them talk.
   310	
   311	The gelding liked the going down no better than the coming up. Lira leaned on his cheek-strap and told him that it was the last hill of the day, while Brom had the tailboard, with one hand on the canvas, and Karis walked behind the cart with her eyes on the load and did not once look back at the roofs.
   312	
   313	At the fourth turn Cael looked back for her. The house stood up against the last of the light, grey and whole, with its gatehouse and its long ranges, and he counted them, since he could not help it: the lecture range, and the residence, and the library, which was the tallest, and at the top of it a small square window.
   314	
   315	There was a lamp in it. It had been lit early, and it was turned up as high as it would go. He did not say so to Karis, for he thought she knew.
   316	
   317	At the road's foot the coats had their two lanterns out, though it was not yet dark. There were two men by the milestone, senior, in travelling grey, with a tin cup on the wall between them. They had stood at that road since before the year opened, and all that time they had been waiting for one thing, which was a word to change in a column. It had changed at a quarter past the first bell.
   318	
   319	The cart came down onto the level and went by them at a walk, and neither man moved. The elder had a walking stick he did not need, and he looked at the horse, and the canvas, and the three shapes under it, and at four people on foot, and at Cael last and longest. There was no ill will in it; he had the face of a man reading the last line of a long ledger. Then he took a book from his coat and a pencil from the book, and wrote.
   320	
   321	Cael could have given him the line, having read enough of the kind on a table at the top of the library. *Subject left the house at dusk, on foot, with three others and a cart. Status changed.* It would be true, every word, and it would go west by the morning bag.
   322	
   323	"He's writing," said Lira, without turning her head.
   324	
   325	"Let him. It's his last page of us."
   326	
   327	"There were two."
   328	
   329	"There'll be one behind us tomorrow," said Cael. "Seln said to expect one. A status goes to an office, and a body gets followed until somebody tells the follower to stop."
   330	
   331	The ferry was waiting, with its lantern lit. The ferryman looked at the cart and named a price, and Brom paid it, and they crossed the Ost on the brown water with the gelding's eyes rolling. On the far bank Ostrand had its windows alight, street above street, and somewhere among them was a wool exchange with a lot number on its floor.
   332	
   333	The woman who kept the guesting-house asked no questions. She had come up the bluff with a basket the day before, and she had seen what there was to see; she gave them the parlour with the two-paned door, and they put the cart in her yard, under the pentice, beside the old screw press.
   334	
   335	He wrote the day's line there, sitting on the press's platen with the book on his knee, where Brom had stood under that beam some days ago and held what it let down on him.
   336	
   337	He had meant to write a good deal. There had been a clock, and a margin, and a key going into a pigeonhole, and a man walking beside a box with nothing in his hand. He looked at the clean leaf, and found that the day had already been written, four times over, by a registrar, and that it wanted very little from him.
   338	
   339	*Seventh-day. Four leaves at Bracken's counter, in the order we came: Lira, Brom, me, Karis. Entered and blotted. Nothing left on the hill with our names on it but what's true.*
   340	
   341	*Four, closed complete. Tomorrow, the fifth.*

