     1	# Chapter 57 — Not At His Desk
     2	
     3	Seln simply wasn't at his desk the next morning.
     4	
     5	The desk clerk opened the assessment wing on the First-day, as he had opened it every working morning for nine years. He hung up his coat. He took the cover off his ledger and sharpened a pencil, and he had the day book open and the date written before he looked round to say good morning to the copying table.
     6	
     7	There was nobody at it.
     8	
     9	That was not, in itself, a thing to write down, for a man could be late. But the clerk sat with his pencil lifted, and found that he could not go on to his column. In two years the teaching assistant had never once been late. He had never been early either. He had come in by the inner door at the same moment each day, without a sound, so that the clerk had mostly not noticed him arrive, and had only looked up and found the table occupied, as one finds that a clock has been wound.
    10	
    11	He got down off his stool and went to look.
    12	
    13	He knew before he had crossed the floor, and it was his ears that told him. For two years a pen had been going at that table from the first bell, a small dry steady sound, and he had added his column against it every morning without once noticing that he did. The room was not making it. He could hear the clock over the counter instead, which he had not heard at that hour since the year before last.
    14	
    15	The table had nothing on it but its blotter, and on the blotter lay four keys.
    16	
    17	They were set in a row, the largest at the left and the smallest at the right, each a finger's width from the next, with their bows all turned the same way. The clerk knew them. There was the key to the inner door, and the key to the press where the cards were kept, and the post room's, which the wing had held since the porter hurt his shoulder. The smallest was the key to the wing's side door. They were every key the house had ever put into that man's hand, and they had been laid out so that the next person would not have to hunt for one.
    18	
    19	There was no note. He looked under the blotter, and in the table's one drawer, and then, feeling foolish, under the table.
    20	
    21	There was a cupboard under the table, which he had never opened, since it was not his. Its door stood an inch ajar. Inside there was nothing whatever but swept boards, and one short end of grey tape.
    22	
    23	He straightened up.
    24	
    25	The stove in the corner was cold. He opened it, since he had opened everything else. It was full to the bars of fine pale ash, as soft as flour. It was a great deal of ash. He had been in the wing on the Sixth-day and the stove had been raked out then, for the bout. He could not think what a man would burn in one mild Seventh-day to make so much, and after a moment he stopped trying, because he found that he did not wish to know.
    26	
    27	On the shelf over the table stood the first manual and the second, and beside them a space. The dust lay a little differently there, in a clean oblong about the length of a forearm, and the clerk looked at it and it told him nothing. He could not have said what had stood in that place. He had sat ten feet from it for two years.
    28	
    29	Above the shelf was the board.
    30	
    31	The wing's weekly summaries hung on it in their rows, from the first week of the year before last to the week just ended, each of them one sheet in the same small square hand, pinned at its two top corners. The floor hours were there, and the cards, and the figures by cohort, and the corrections, with a margin wherever a correction had been made and the reason for it. The last sheet was dated the Seventh-day. It was finished, and totalled, and ruled off.
    32	
    33	The clerk read that last sheet through, standing, and there was not an error in it. There had never been an error in any of them. He had been corrected by that hand himself, once, in a margin, in the seventh week of this year. *Both seen*, the margin had said, and he had been angry for a day and then grateful.
    34	
    35	He did not take the summaries down, and nobody ever did.
    36	
    37	He went to the Magister at the second bell and was told to write what he had found and nothing he supposed. He wrote *The teaching assistant not at his table. Keys left on the blotter, four. No word.* The Magister read it and wrote something in his small book, and sent him across the court to the registrar.
    38	
    39	A new assistant came to the wing in the next intake, and worked under that board without remarking on it, and another came after her. Eleven years later the desk clerk was the wing's senior man, with a stool of better leather and a boy to sharpen his pencils, and the sheets were still pinned there in their rows, gone the colour of weak tea. He would not have them moved. He could not have told anybody why, and he said only, when he was asked, that they were correct.
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	The registrar did what a registrar does.
    44	
    45	A man on the house's strength had not come to his work. He was not an enrollee, whose leaving had a form, and he was not faculty, whose leaving had a council. He had been placed with the wing two years ago by a district office's letter, as teaching assistants were, and it was to that office that the house now owed a question. Bracken wrote it before noon, in his upright hand, with one pair of glasses on, and he put no adjective in it, for he had none he could prove. It went down the hill in the First-day bag.
    46	
    47	*Teaching assistant, Bronze grade, fifteen years' service, absent without notice from first bell of the First-day. Residence room found vacated; no personal effects; bed made. All working files completed and cross-indexed to date. Inner-door key, records-press key, post-room key and wing side-door key surrendered on the copying-table blotter. Floor schedule posted one week forward. No note left, no leave applied for, no address of forwarding held by this house. The house records no disciplinary matter, no irregularity of any kind in the two years of this posting, and no view as to cause. Query submitted to the placing district office per code; direction requested.*
    48	
    49	It was a single leaf, and it asked where a man was. Leaves of that kind go into a tray, and somebody takes them out in their turn, and looks up the name.
    50	
    51	This one was looked up. The name on it stood in a district's roll of placed staff, with a posting against it and a date. The clerk who found it there would ordinarily have answered within the week: *not recalled by this office; no instruction; the house may fill the place.* He began to write exactly that.
    52	
    53	But the roll had a mark against the name. It was a small mark, in the margin, of a kind that he saw perhaps twice in a year, and it did not tell him what it meant; it told him only that the leaf in his hand was not his to answer. He stopped writing. He put the query into a second envelope, sealed it, and sent it on.
    54	
    55	So it came, some days after, to another desk.
    56	
    57	Nothing can be said of that desk. It had no board beside its door, and its building was like the buildings on either side of it, and the person who sat at it was neither cruel nor kind in any way that the work required. There was a drawer, and in the drawer were the postings of a number of quiet people, and against each posting a bundle of what that person had sent in.
    58	
    59	The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
    60	
    61	There was none after it.
    62	
    63	So there were two absences, and they had come within one week: a man was not at his table, and a return had not been written. The desk laid the two facts side by side, as it had been trained to, and looked at what lay between them. It had before it the house's query, in a registrar's honest hand, and a seat's ruling, and a district officer's paper endorsed *no such enrollee*. It had a watcher's line from the foot of a road, which said that a subject had gone down at dusk with three others and a cart.
    64	
    65	*Three others.* The line said nothing of a fifth.
    66	
    67	The desk wrote a question on a slip, and it was a short one.
    68	
    69	*What did he have?*
    70	
    71	It was the right question, the one any careful officer asks when a quiet man goes. Nobody at that desk thought he had taken anything. He had sat at a copying table, and held four keys, and laid all four out in a row. But for two years he had been closer to one subject than anybody else in the service, and before that he had been close to sixty-two others, and he had watched, and written what he saw.
    72	
    73	So the desk asked for what he had written.
    74	
    75	All of it was called up. A requisition went out to nineteen districts, for every return under that name, from the first posting to the last, and they came in over the following weeks, bundle after bundle, tied in the office's grey tape. Readers were set to them. The readers were patient people, and they knew their work, which was to find the place where a man's account went thin.
    76	
    77	They read fifteen years of it, and every line was true.
    78	
    79	They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
    80	
    81	It was also empty.
    82	
    83	That took them longer to see, for the returns were so full of true things, of hours and figures and the names of rooms. But when the readers had finished, they found that they held fifteen years of a man's eyes, and they could not have said what one of his sixty-three subjects was like, or what any of them had wanted, or feared, or carried. He had reported everything that could be checked, and he had left out all the rest, and he had done it so evenly, for so long, that it had been taken for a style.
    84	
    85	The desk had its answer then, or the only one it would get.
    86	
    87	It knew what he had sent. It did not know what he had kept. There was no return that listed the things a quiet man had declined to write down. Nor was there any record of a records room on a top floor, in a district he had left twelve years before, where he had gone to look for a subject's travel papers. Nobody thought to ask for one.
    88	
    89	The slip with its question stayed on the file. It was never struck through.
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	He had begun on the stove at the first bell.
    94	
    95	It was a mild day for a fire, and Seln had lit one anyway, as soon as the desk clerk's stool stood empty for the Seventh-day. There were nineteen bundles in the cupboard under the copying table, standing on their ends in the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from a single posting. He had carried them from town to town for fifteen years and had never been able to say what for. A man who copied his mother's ledgers at a copper a page did not learn afterward to keep only one of a thing.
    96	
    97	He fed them in a posting at a time, in the order he had served them.
    98	
    99	He did not read them. There was nothing in them to read; he had seen to that when he wrote them. He untied each tape, and laid the leaves in by the handful, and waited until they had gone grey before he put in the next. A river town went first, where he had learned to fold a return. Then a guild hall, and a district seat in an old building, which he fed in rather quickly. There was an academy with four subjects, and a station in the hills with a green corridor.
   100	
   101	Between bundles he copied the wing's forms, since a man sitting idle beside a stove is a thing a porter remembers.
   102	
   103	The boy came to the counter at the third bell with a card he had no need to bring. Seln took it and gave him a line on a floor sheet, and put the sheet where the bundles were going. He thought the boy had seen the ash. He thought, too, that the boy had understood it, and would write none of it down, and two years ago he could not have relied on that. It was a strange thing to have taught somebody, and stranger to be glad of it.
   104	
   105	The last bundle was the thickest. It had the name of this hill on its label, and its count said *one*.
   106	
   107	He held it a little longer than the rest. It was two years of true weeks. *Subject attended. Subject's figure as entered by the house.* He had been proud of the others once, and there was a commendation for them in a drawer. He found that he was not proud of this one. He was something else, and he did not have the word, and the catechism had never supplied it.
   108	
   109	He put it in, and shut the door on it.
   110	
   111	After the tenth bell he laid out the keys. There were four, and he set them on the blotter in a row, by size, and turned the bows one way. It was not a message. It was a courtesy, to a clerk who added his column aloud and would be alarmed in the morning, since a man who finds the keys does not have to wonder what has been taken, and need only count them.
   112	
   113	He did not write a letter. There was nobody to address it to who could be told the truth, and he had filed enough true, empty paper for one life. He had said the whole of it at a table two nights ago, to four people, and it had been written under a line with no name at its head, and that was all the filing it would get.
   114	
   115	He took his coat from its peg. He put the strap of the post satchel over his head, so that the weight lay against his hip, and he went out by the wing's side door and drew it shut until the latch dropped.
   116	
   117	He did not go by the gate. The lodge-man kept a column for every bell, and Seln had read that column on a good many Seventh-days. He went by the stable passage and the farm track, as he had taken four people on a night in the recess, through an orchard that was black and smelled of wet bark. He walked down the middle of the track, where the grass was shortest, and at the gardener's gate he lifted the latch and let it down behind him without a click. It swung true; the large one had mended its top hinge with a mallet and a wedge.
   118	
   119	There was a jar on the sill of the gardener's cottage. He knew it was there, though he could not see it, and he knew what was in it. He had counted five beans onto that man's table once and called it arithmetic, and had not expected the gardener to keep them.
   120	
   121	He did not take one. A thing left where it lay was sometimes all the mark a man could make.
   122	
   123	At the meadow gate he stopped, by habit, to count the people behind him by touch. He remembered, with his hand already lifted, that there were none.
   124	
   125	---
   126	
   127	The bridge-ward knew him.
   128	
   129	For two years he had fetched the house's pouch across that bridge, at the end of a day, with the same satchel on the same shoulder. He nodded to the ward's lantern and was nodded to, and went over the middle arch above the brown water, and nobody would think of it again. The two coats were at the bluff road's foot and the two at the landing, where they had been set to stand, and they had not been set to watch a clerk go for the post.
   130	
   131	He took the upper lane through the town, behind the exchange, where the windows were dark. In one of those streets four people were asleep, or lying awake, with a cart in a yard. Beyond the last houses the east road began, and the first of its stones.
   132	
   133	He walked it in the dark at the pace he had taught, which was not fast, and never stopped where there was a wall. The satchel knocked against his hip at each step. He had carried the thing inside it for twelve years, and had never before carried it at night, on a road, toward anybody.
   134	
   135	It was a surveyor's case. He had bought it in his fifth posting, from a maker who built them for men who took levels across wet country. It had a brass lock and no mark outside, and inside the lid a small stamp, a pair of dividers, which he had looked at perhaps ten thousand times. Before the case there had been an oilcloth and a boot-box. Before that, for five weeks, there had been only a floor, and one lamp, and a press whose doors he shut each morning before the building woke.
   136	
   137	He had not known what he was copying, though the few who learned of it assumed otherwise. He had gone to that press for a subject's travel endorsements, which had been filed where they did not belong, and he had found paper that was correct in every particular and sat a little above where it should have sat; and he had copied it because he copied things. Understanding had come a year later, in a station corridor painted green, and it had come all at once, so that he had stood very still against a wall while a keeper went by with a tray.
   138	
   139	After that he had kept it.
   140	
   141	*A thing kept is a wall.* An old woman with a stick had taught him that, over a chandler's, with a cat on her knee. *A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.* He had said those lines on nineteen first mornings. He had kept the case shut for twelve years and it had been a very good wall: it had stood between him and every person he was ever sent to watch, and he had sat behind it and written true things, and been safe.
   142	
   143	He had spent it in a reading room, on a table, in front of four young people, and again in a borrowed office, in front of a man at a window.
   144	
   145	So it was a door now, and it had opened the other way as well, as she had said it would. The wall was gone, and they could reach him. They knew his cup, and what he did with his hands, and one of them had come back up a road in the dark to look for him.
   146	
   147	He had not been looked for in a good many years. He had found it harder to bear than being watched.
   148	
   149	The road climbed. He felt it in his calves before he saw it, a long easy rise out of the river's flat, and at the top of it the hedges fell away on either side.
   150	
   151	He stopped there.
   152	
   153	He knew better. It was the first thing he had told them in the orchard, and he had made each of them say it back. You did not halt on a crest. You did not stand in the middle of a road with the sky behind you, where any child could count you. You went over the top without turning your head, and a hundred yards on, where the ground had dropped, you could look from behind something if you must.
   154	
   155	Seln stood on the crest, in the middle of the road, and turned all the way round.
   156	
   157	He did it badly. He knew he was doing it badly, and he did not move. He had left nineteen towns and had not looked back at one of them. It had not been a hardship. There had been nothing behind him in any of those places but a file and a rented room, and he had no practice at this. He stood square, like a first-year told to be seen, with his hands hanging.
   158	
   159	The valley was a darkness with a lighter band in it, which was the river. Beyond the river the bluff stood up, blacker than the sky, and he found the house by its shape. It had no lights, for it was past midnight, and four hundred people were asleep in it.
   160	
   161	It had one light.
   162	
   163	It was high up, at the top of the tallest roof, a small square of yellow, turned up as high as a wick would go. He had sat under that lamp on the evening of a birthday, at a table with a honey cake on it, and said that it was a good room. It had been kept burning every night since, so that the valley would see what it always saw.
   164	
   165	But the room was empty tonight. He knew where every person was who had ever turned that wick, and so somebody else had lit it.
   166	
   167	He stood and worked it through, since that was his trade. It would be the girl with ink on her collar. She would have asked nobody. She knew nothing, and had lit it on no instruction, and she would go on lighting it without knowing why. And every night it burned, the people at the road's foot would write that the room at the top of the library was occupied as usual.
   168	
   169	He had spent fifteen years making true reports that told nothing. A second-year had just done it by accident, with a lamp, because she missed somebody.
   170	
   171	"Mm," said Seln, aloud, on the crest.
   172	
   173	He looked for as long as it takes to say a short thing twice. Then he turned east and went down the far side, and he did not look round again, having found that once was a great deal.
   174	
   175	---
   176	
   177	He came to the eighteenth stone a little after it was light.
   178	
   179	There was a crossroads beyond it. A lane ran north to a farm and another south toward the river, and in the angle of them lay a piece of rough common with a thorn brake along one side, and a stream that went under the south lane through a pipe of fired clay. He had passed the place twice in two years, on the house's errands, and had marked it each time for no reason he would have admitted.
   180	
   181	He walked the ground first, as he did with any ground.
   182	
   183	The brake would hide a fire from the road until a traveller was nearly on it, and that was to the good. The common drained, and the grass was cropped; sheep had been on it, and not lately. He went to the stream and stood on its bank, and then he turned his back to the water and began to pace.
   184	
   185	Forty paces. It was his own rule, and he had given it in an orchard with the reasons: water covers a voice at forty paces and a foot at ten, and things walk to water at night. He had told them it was to be forty paces of the shortest person present, and the researcher had written in her notebook, quite seriously, *of whose?*
   186	
   187	So he shortened his stride to hers. He had watched her cross a court often enough to know it. It brought him to a level place under the brake's lee, with a flat stone in it, and he looked at that and found nothing wrong.
   188	
   189	He set the satchel down against the stone, and slept two hours with his back to it, sitting, as he had slept in coaches. When he woke, the sun had come round, and he began on the wood.
   190	
   191	It took him the rest of the day, and that surprised him. He had supposed that a man gathered sticks for an hour and had done. He had not built a fire out of doors since he was a boy on a quay, and in nineteen postings every hearth he sat at had been lit by somebody whose work it was. He went along the brake and found that most of what lay under it was rotten, and that what was sound was green. He went up the north lane as far as a fallen ash and broke off what he could with his hands and his boot. He carried it back in his arms, four journeys, and each time he came round the thorn he looked at the heap and thought it small.
   192	
   193	There would be five of them, and one fire.
   194	
   195	He kept arriving at that as he worked. They would have come from first light with a loaded cart, and a ford, and a horse none of them knew. The boy would have an arm he could not close and two days of bills come due at once, and the girl would have talked to the horse the whole way and would be hoarse. They would come round that thorn at dusk, tired through, and cold, since people are always cold at the end of the first day.
   196	
   197	He had spent his working life at the edge of other people's tables. He had been fed at them, and had liked a great many of the people he sat among, and had written every one of them down afterward, in the old pattern, and sent it up. He had never once got anywhere first, and made it ready, for people who would be glad to find it so.
   198	
   199	He broke the ash across his knee and laid it small to large, as a quay-man had shown him forty years ago. He was absurdly careful about it.
   200	
   201	There was one thing left over from the old life, and it came at its hour.
