   307	The river came down brown, forty feet across, with a ripple at the middle where the bed dropped. There was no keeper's hut on the near side, only a post with marks cut in it to show the depth, and the water stood a hand below the mark that somebody had painted red. A chain ran across, bank to bank, on iron stanchions, for a man on foot to hold.
   308	
   309	Lira looked at the red mark, and then at the gelding.
   310	
   311	"He'll go," she said. "He won't like it."
   312	
   313	"The boxes," said Karis. It was the first time her voice had gone thin all day. "How deep is the bed of the cart?"
   314	
   315	Brom was already on his knees at the wheel. He measured the axle against his own thigh, and then he waded in, boots and all, to the middle, and stood where the ripple was, with the water at his hip. He waded back.
   316	
   317	"A hand under the bed," he said. "If he doesn't stop. If he stops in the middle, the stream'll bank up against the wheel, and then it's over the boards." He looked at Lira. "Can you keep him walking?"
   318	
   319	"I can keep him walking."
   320	
