    42	
    43	"Every second day," said Karis, breathing hard. She had stopped beside it with her wedge. "I said so, and I was guessing. It may be every tenth, up here. It may be when somebody remembers." She touched the bare hook with one pale finger. "I wonder who decided. Somebody sat at a desk and decided that past this post a letter wasn't worth a horse."
    44	
    45	"Somebody added it up," said Brom. "It'll have been right."
    46	
    47	"I'm sure it was right. That's what I mind."
    48	
    49	Behind them, three hundred yards down the slope, the grey rider had got off his cob and was leading it. He kept his distance on foot exactly as he had kept it mounted, and when they stopped, he stopped, and let the horse crop.
    50	
    51	They came to the top in the last of the light. The land lay over on its back there, wide and bare and rolling, with the wind going across it, and half an hour on stood a low building with a lamp in its window.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	It was the last chartered waystation, and it had the sign to prove it.
    56	
    57	The board hung from an iron arm over the door, with the registry's seal burned into it, and the wind had swung it against the wall so often that it had worn a pale groove in the stone. There was a yard with a wall round it, and a well with a lid, and a stack of cut turf against the gable, higher than a man.
