   261	"I'm Copper on that side of this stone," he said. "For good. That's in a book, and it'll never be altered, because I'll never sign. I've known it since a landing, and I wanted to say it once while I was still standing where it's true." He drew a breath. "On this side there's no book. So I don't know what I am over here. I expect I'll find out by lifting things."
   262	
   263	He stepped across, and the off wheel followed him over.
   264	
   265	Cael was the last. He did not go straight over. He went to the frame-house door and pushed it with his sound hand, and it swung in on dry hinges.
   266	
   267	The plate lay in the floor, in the middle of the room, under its frame. It was brass, and it had gone the brown of an old coin, and there was dust on it, for the carters who swept the house had left this room alone. Nothing in it stirred when he stood in the door, and he had not thought that it would.
   268	
   269	He had been fourteen, in a room like this, at the far end of the country, and had put his hand where he was shown.
   270	
   271	"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
   272	
   273	He pulled the door to. He did not latch it; it had not been latched.
   274	
   275	Then he walked back to the track, and stepped over the stone.
   276	
   277	---
   278	
   279	"It's a morning's walk," said Karis. "That's the wording. *The bound of a station is a morning's walk from its sigil.* A keeper walked it once, I suppose, and came home for his dinner. Nobody ever wrote down where he turned."
   280	
   281	"I know where," said Lira.
   282	
   283	She had the carter's book open on her forearm as she walked. She had it written there, from the words of a young man on a ferry landing who would not say why.
   284	
   285	"He told me exactly. There's a swell in the ground, and a thorn with a split trunk, and after the thorn the track goes down a little. That's all. He said there's no fence, and no post. He said you'd walk past it if nobody told you."
   286	
   287	"And he turns there."
   288	
   289	"He turns there. So do others. He said I'd know it by that."
   290	
   291	They came to it a little before noon. There was the swell, and the thorn; and beside the track, on the left, the turf was cut to pieces.
   292	
   293	It was a wide ring of churned ground, thirty feet across, trodden bare. Wheel ruts swung off the track into it, and round, and back onto the track facing west, a great many of them, one over another, old ones grassed and new ones sharp. It was where the wagons turned. Every carter who would not go on had come this far, and put his team about, and gone home, and they had worn it into the hill.
   294	
   295	Beyond the ring two pairs of ruts went on.
   296	
   297	That was the whole of it. The registry had set no stone here, and cut no device. The only mark on the ground had been made by the people who stopped.
   298	
   299	They stood at the ring's far edge, with the gelding blowing a little, and Cael waited for the thing in him to ask. It asked at every threshold. It had asked at gates and doorways and the lip of a ford, and at a sill with two sockets, that very morning; and he stood with his boots at the place where a carter said the world's paper ended, and listened for it.
   300	
   301	It said nothing.
   302	
   303	There was no threshold. It knew a threshold when it met one, better than he did, and it had found none here, only grass, and then more grass. He had been told for a year that there was a line across this road, and he had half believed he would feel it, as one feels a draught. The line was in a code, and in a carter's head, and in the ruts. It was not in the ground at all.
   304	
   305	He stepped forward, and it was a step like any other, and the rest came after him, with the cart.
   306	
   307	Seln had stopped.
   308	
   309	He stood in the track, ten paces on, and he had turned round. He did it properly this time: he had put the thorn between himself and the sky, and he looked back along the road they had come by, from the near ground outward, in sections, as Cael had been taught to look in an orchard. He took a long time over it.
   310	
   311	"Mm," he said.
   312	
   313	"Well?" said Lira.
   314	
   315	"I have looked down a great many roads," said Seln. "I looked on behalf of the people who had put somebody on them, and I always knew where he would stand, because I should have stood there myself." He did not take his eyes off the track. "I find that I do not altogether trust what I see. I have wanted it too long. I should be obliged if somebody else would say it."
   316	
   317	Brom let go of the gelding's rein.
   318	
   319	He walked back to where Seln stood, and looked west, with his arms hanging, as he looked at a rack of plates. The track ran back across the upland, pale, as far as the roofs of the station, with the water beyond, and the bare rise. There was no rider on it. There was no lantern, and no tin cup, and no man with a pencil on a top tier.
   320	
   321	He counted. Cael saw his lips move. Then he began at the near end and counted again.
   322	
   323	"Nobody's watching," said Lira.
   324	
   325	It was very quiet, for the wind had dropped.
   326	
   327	Seln sat down. He did it without any warning; he sat on the bank beside the track, with the satchel in his lap, like a man who has been standing at a counter for a very long time, and he put both his hands flat on the leather.
   328	
   329	Lira was turning round, slowly, on one heel, with her arms a little out. She went all the way round, as he had seen her do on a tier when she counted twelve hundred faces, and there was no face. She completed the circle and stopped, and did not seem to know what to do with her arms.
   330	
   331	"I thought I'd want to shout," she said. "I've nothing to shout at."
   332	
   333	"Enter the time," said Karis. Her voice was not steady. "Somebody ought to. What's the hour?"
   334	
   335	Cael took Hesk's clock out of its stocking and held it up.
   336	
   337	"A quarter to eleven. By this."
   338	
   339	"That clock loses."
   340	
   341	"It always has."
   342	
   343	"Then by what do we—" Karis stopped. She looked at the sky, which had no bell in it, and at the track, which had no board of hours. "There isn't anything else, is there. That's the only clock east of here."
   344	
   345	"It's the only one I know of."
   346	
   347	"Then it's right," said Karis. She opened her notebook with her pale fingers and wrote the figure down. "By definition. I shall have to think about that."
   348	
   349	---
   350	
   351	It began to rain an hour after.
   352	
   353	It came out of the west, behind them, and it stayed. By the middle of the afternoon the track was running, and by dusk they could not see a hundred yards. There was no thorn up there, and no wall. Brom chocked the cart on the lee side of a low bank and they ran the canvas out from its bed on two of the gelding's hobble-stakes, and all five of them got in under it, with the boxes.
   354	
   355	There was no fire, for there was nothing on that upland to burn that was not soaked. They ate what the last keeper had sold them, cold. Seln named the watches, two and two, and Brom took the first with Lira, and the dusk came down grey.
   356	
   357	The quiet thing stirred in it, as it always did at that hour. Cael felt it lift, soft, behind everything else he carried, and he held it where it was. It had been sealed from anybody who could carry the seeing west. He looked out under the canvas's edge at a hundred yards of rain, and thought that there was now nobody within three days who could carry anything anywhere, and that the seal's own terms had very nearly run out.
   358	
   359	He left it sealed. He had not come all this way to try a door on the first night because it was unwatched.
   360	
   361	It was the first night of his life outside any jurisdiction, and he lay under a cart and waited to see what that was like. It was wet, and his arm ached. Lira, on watch, was telling the gelding that he had been perfectly right about the hill. Karis had her back against a box and her knees drawn up. Nobody on the continent was required to write a line about any of it.
   362	
   363	"I want a light," he said. "A small one. For a quarter of an hour."
   364	
   365	Karis looked at him. Then she put out her left hand, to the cart's tailboard, and touched the wet oak with one finger, and a flame stood up from it, no bigger than a pin's head. It sat on the wood without marking it, steady, and gave about as much light as would show a page.
   366	
   367	"That's all I have that won't scorch," she said. "Write small."
   368	
   369	He took Hesk's book from the satchel. He did not turn to the day's leaf. He put two fingers under the back board, where the leather had been cut to make a pocket, and drew out a letter.
   370	
   371	It was soft at the folds by now. It had come up a bluff in a post-room sort in the year's seventh week, and he had read it some dozens of times, at a sill, and knew it without the flame. A Gold at Auremont had written to tell him not to mistake what was happening to him for procedure. She had said that the rematch stood, and she had told him to keep himself unclassified.
   372	
   373	He had not answered, for there had been no answer he could put into a pouch that a clerk would read for completeness. There was no pouch out here. He tore a clean leaf from the back of the observation notebook and laid it on the book's cover, under the pin of light.
   374	
   375	*They ruled. We left — resigned, before the date, in good standing, four files closed complete and one man who filed nothing. You said keep myself unclassified. Done, permanently, on my own paper this time: I'm past the Registry Line, where the word has no meaning, which turns out to be the only place the word is safe.*
   376	
   377	*The rematch stands. Not postponed — relocated. It happens on a floor nobody owns, and I think you'd prefer that anyway; a Gold-tier fact and an unclassified one, fighting where no seal can score either of us, is the only honest version of the bout, and we both knew it at Norhold. Find me when you're ready to fight something no ladder built. I'll be east.*
   378	
   379	*— C.*
   380	
   381	He read it over once. It was true, and there was nothing in it that he would have minded a clerk copying. He folded it in three and wrote her name on the outside, and *Auremont*, and nothing else.
   382	
   383	"Is that her?" said Lira, from the dark. She had come in from the horse, with rain on her face. "The Gold?"
   384	
   385	"Yes."
   386	
   387	"What did you tell her?"
   388	
   389	"That it stands."
   390	
   391	"Good," said Lira. "I'd like to see that one from the east side."
   392	
   393	Karis closed her finger and thumb, and the flame went out.
   394	
   395	The rain had gone by morning, and the stream at the foot of the slope had come up. It was the first water on that road with no post beside it and no red mark, and it ran wide over stones, the colour of beer, fast and loud and not deep.
   396	
   397	A man was sitting on the far side.
   398	
   399	He sat on a rock above the water with a mule beside him, eating something out of a cloth, and he had two leather bags across the mule's back, flat, with long flaps. He looked at them across the stream without getting up. He had clearly been there some time, waiting for the water to fall, and was in no hurry about it.
   400	
   401	"That's a post-rider," said Seln. "Not the registry's. There is no lead on the bags."
   402	
   403	"Out here?"
   404	
   405	"There are holds out here, and people in them who have cousins in the west, and somebody will always carry a letter for a copper. He will take them to the old man at the turf fire, who marks them, and they go down the hill when there is a bag." Seln studied the man a moment longer. "He is going where we have come from. I think he is the first person we have met who is."
   406	
   407	They put the cart across. It was not the brown river over again, for the water was nowhere above the knee; but the bed was all loose stone, and the bank on the far side was broken, and it took them the better part of an hour. The near wheel went down between two rocks and would not come out, and Brom got under the hub with his back and lifted, without any grey in his arms at all, while Seln and Karis dragged a flat stone under the rim. Lira said a number of things to the gelding. The man on the far bank finished what was in his cloth, and watched, and offered no opinion.
   408	
   409	When they came up, streaming, he got off his rock.
   410	
   411	"It'll be down by noon," he said. "I'd have waited, in your place."
   412	
   413	"We've a hill," said Lira.
   414	
   415	"You have. I've just come down it." He looked at the gelding without enthusiasm. "He'll not thank you."
   416	
   417	Cael had the letter inside his coat, dry, and he took it out.
   418	
   419	"You're going west. Will you carry one?"
   420	
   421	"That's what I'm for." The man took it and read the outside, slowly, with his lips.
   422	
   423	Cael looked at him doing it. He had lain under a cart last night and reckoned that there was nobody within three days who could carry a thing west, and he had been wrong by one night and a mule. He was glad that he had left a certain door shut.
   424	
   425	"Auremont," said the post-rider. "That's a long way for a copper."
   426	
   427	"It's a copper as far as the waystation. He'll mark it there."
   428	
   429	"He will. He marks anything. I've seen him mark a ham." The man turned the letter over, and there was nothing on the back of it. "No house on it. Where's she to write to, if she's a mind?"
   430	
   431	"She isn't to write. She's only to know."
   432	
   433	The man thought that over, and seemed to find nothing wrong with it. He put the letter into the nearer bag, under the flap, among a dozen others, and buckled it. Brom paid him. He put his foot in the mule's stirrup and swung up, and looked once at the cart, and the canvas, and the five of them standing wet on a bank.
   434	
   435	"Boxes," he said, to nobody, and shook his head. He rode down into the stream, which he had been waiting all morning to see fall, and went through it at a walk and up the far side, and he did not look back either. Nobody out here seemed to.
   436	
   437	Cael watched the bag go. It had her name in it. It would go to a turf fire, and down a hill, and onto a hook, and it would cross the whole of a country that had a file on him, and come at last to a hall in a city, and to a woman for whom a ladder had been built to exactly her own height.
   438	
   439	He sat down on the bank with Hesk's book on his knee. It was his log, where he set down what he carried and what he owed. One promise had gone west that minute in a mule's bag, and it was answered, not paid. The other was older, and nobody had been written to about it at all. He put the two side by side on the page.
   440	
   441	The log banked the thread beside its lineage:
   442	
   443	*Reydan wanted the answer when I have it. Daeva wants the rematch with whatever I am. Both promises ride east with me. Both get kept. Not yet. The road's long and it runs the right direction.*
   444	
   445	---
   446	
   447	The hill began at the stream's edge.
   448	
   449	It was not like the long climb, which had gone up all day, round the land's shoulders. This went straight. The track took a ridge by its nose and climbed it in a single pull, between rocks, with the ground falling off on either hand.
   450	
   451	The gelding gave it a third of the way.
   452	
   453	Then he stopped, and it was not the stopping of a clock. His head was down between his knees and his sides went in and out like a smith's bellows, and there was a rattle in his breath that had not been there on the upland. Lira had her hand on his neck, and she did not say one word to him.
   454	
   455	"He's blowing," she said. "That's not temper. That's all he has."
   456	
   457	"Chock her," said Brom, and Karis had the wedge under the wheel already.
   458	
   459	They stood on the ridge in the wind, the five of them, and looked at an old brown horse. He had been bought for a hill a day, and he had done one each day, honestly, for five days, and this was a second in one morning, after a river.
   460	
   461	"Boxes off," said Brom.
   462	
   463	"All three?"
   464	
   465	"All three. It's the weight on his back end, going up. We take them up by hand, and the cart goes up light, and they go on again at the top."
   466	
   467	"That's three trips," said Karis faintly. "Each."
   468	
   469	"It's what I do," said Brom.
   470	
   471	He took the first. Seln and Lira took the second between them, by its ropes, and it was the first time Brom had let anybody touch one; he looked at them doing it, and said nothing, and went on. Karis led the gelding, with the empty cart rattling behind, and talked to him in a small dry voice about the provisions of a code he would never need.
   472	
   473	Cael carried the wedge.
   474	
   475	It was all he could carry. He went up behind the wheel with a block of ash in his sound hand, and each time the gelding stopped he put it down. He had stood on a stone floor against a Gold, in front of eight thousand people, and he had gone down the Crown yard on a river of dust; and he climbed the first ridge east of the Line stopping a cart from rolling backward, and it was the most useful thing he did that day.
   476	
   477	They came to the top a little after noon.
   478	
   479	He did not see it coming. The track went up between two rocks, and then there was no more up, and the wind hit him in the face. He walked forward three paces to get out of the cart's way, and looked.
   480	
   481	He had thought he knew what large was. He had seen the continent drawn on a sheet. He had crossed a good part of it, at a wagon's pace, to stand in a hall at Norhold, and he had looked down from a bluff at a river valley every day for two years and thought it a wide thing.
   482	
   483	The land in front of him went on until it was sky.
   484	
   485	There was a valley below, deep and long, with a thread of water in it. Beyond the valley was another ridge, higher than this, dark with trees he could not name from here, and beyond that another, paler, and another after that, the colour of smoke; he counted seven before the air took them. There was no road on any of it but the two ruts at his feet. He saw no smoke, and no roof. The wind came over it from somewhere that nobody had drawn, and it smelled of nothing he knew.
   486	
   487	Nobody spoke. Brom had put the first box down and was standing with his hands open, and Karis had let go of the horse.
   488	
   489	"Oh," said Lira, very quietly, beside him.
   490	
   491	He sat down on the rock at the track's edge, and took the book out, and the pencil. His hand was not quite steady. He wrote the entry at once, before he could make it into more than it was.
   492	
   493	*First look at the edge territories. Everyone warns you the world out here isn't governed. Nobody mentions that it's big.*

