     1	# Chapter 60 — Nine
     2	
     3	The boxes went down the far side of the ridge before the cart did.
     4	
     5	Brom would have it no other way, and Lira agreed with him for the gelding's sake.
     6	
     7	"He doesn't like going down," she said. "I've told you that. He likes it less than going up."
     8	
     9	"Then he doesn't go down with a load pushing him," said Brom.
    10	
    11	It was the worst ground of the week. The whole face of the hill was broken stone, grey and loose, lying at the angle where it had come to rest and no steeper, and the track had been scraped across it with a mattock, a cart's width and no more, turning back on itself wherever it ran out of hill. On the outside there was nothing but air.
    12	
    13	So they did it by hand again, the other way, a leg at a time. Brom carried the first box down to the turn and came back up for the third, as he had carried them out of a gate, and Seln and Karis took the second between them. Then he ran an ash pole from the crossroads through the spokes of both wheels, so that they could not turn, and the empty cart went down on its locked tyres like a sledge. He walked behind it with a rope round his waist and the rope round the axle, leaning back, while Lira had the gelding's head and the scree moved under every hoof. At the turn the boxes were taken up again. Cael went ahead of them all with the wedge and the hobble-stakes, and at each bend he put his back against the hill and told them where the edge was.
    14	
    15	It asked him about that edge. It had been quiet since the station, the quick one, and he had thought it was done. But there had never been an edge like this, which ran for four hundred yards with a hundred feet of nothing on the far side of it.
    16	
    17	*Edge?*
    18	
    19	*Yes*, he told it. *This time, yes. That one's an edge. Keep off it.*
    20	
    21	It was the first time he had ever agreed with it, and it seemed satisfied.
    22	
    23	Partway down they came through water. A seep ran out of the hill above the track and across it, and the stones there were black and slid like wet slates, so that Brom took the cart over them a hand's breadth at a time.
    24	
    25	A little below the wet place the track bent round a single block of stone the size of a barn. It had come down off the ridge in some year before there was a road, and stuck, and everything that fell after it had banked up behind it and gone to turf. A twisted pine had got its roots into the crack between the block and the slope. There was a flat there, thirty paces of it, with the great stone standing between it and the drop, and under the pine the ground was dry.
    26	
    27	Brom stopped the cart. He stood leaning back on the rope, with the sweat running off his jaw, and looked the place over from one end to the other.
    28	
    29	"Here," he said.
    30	
    31	"There's two hours of light yet," said Lira.
    32	
    33	"There's as much hill again under us, and that horse has done two climbs and a river since he ate. I've carried a box up a ridge and down one." He let the rope go slack. "Here."
    34	
    35	Nobody argued. They looked at Seln, from habit, and he was already walking the ground.
    36	
    37	There was nothing on it. Cael saw that as he stood with the stakes in his hand. Every place they had slept that week had been made by somebody: a woman had kept a parlour, and a narrow man a long room, and an old man had cut turf; even at the last station there had been a roof, hung on a carter's wire, and at the crossroads a fire had been waiting. This was a shelf of turf behind a fallen rock, with a pine tree on it. Whatever it was going to be by dark, they would have to make it.
    38	
    39	Seln had begun on the wood.
    40	
    41	He did not ask anybody. He went round the foot of the block, where the pine had been dropping dead limbs for longer than there had been a road, and gathered them up, and broke them across his knee and laid them small to large. He was quicker at it than he had been four days ago, and he had the fire's place chosen, in the lee of the stone, before the gelding was out of the shafts.
    42	
    43	"Water," said Karis. She had her notebook out. "That seep we came through."
    44	
    45	She went back up the track to it, and came down again frowning at her own figures.
    46	
    47	"It's thirty-six paces, of mine, and I did it twice. There's nowhere on this flat that's forty from it."
    48	
    49	They all looked at Seln. He straightened up from the fire with a stick in each hand, and considered the flat, and the pine, and the track going up.
    50	
    51	"The rule is for water that covers a voice," he said. "And for water that things walk down to at night. That is a seep on a scree. It would not cover a cough, and nothing comes to it but us." He bent again. "A rule has its reasons written beside it so that one may tell when they are absent. I should stay where you are."
    52	
    53	Karis wrote that down too.
    54	
    55	He put a light to the fire as the sun went off the shelf, and it caught at once. The dead pine was full of resin, and it burned with a clear high flame and a smell Cael had never met, and he stood and watched it take.
    56	
    57	It was only a fire. But no keeper had laid it, and it stood on no map; it burned on the second day past the Line, above a valley that had no name on any sheet, and no Arbiter had ever measured the ground it stood on.
    58	
    59	---
    60	
    61	"It's your turn," said Karis.
    62	
    63	She said it to Seln. She had the rota in her notebook. He had given them the rule himself, in an orchard: two watches of four hours, two to a watch, and the fifth sleeps through, and the fifth changes each night. She had ruled a table for it on the first evening, by a crossroads fire, with five initials down its side.
    64	
    65	"Lira slept through at the crossroads," she said. "Brom at the first waystation, though nobody watched there. I did, on the hill, and Cael at the station. Last night nobody slept at all, under that cart, so I haven't counted it. It's you."
    66	
    67	"I think not, tonight."
    68	
    69	"It's in the table."
    70	
    71	"I do not doubt the table." Seln turned his cup a quarter turn. "I doubt the ground. We are in country none of us has seen, and there is a fire, which can be seen from that ridge and from the one beyond it. Every other night there has been a wall, or a watcher whose habits I knew." He looked at the dark coming up out of the valley. "I have been trained to this. The rest of you have had it from me in an orchard, for a fortnight. I should prefer to be awake."
    72	
    73	"No," said Lira.
    74	
    75	She had her boots off and her feet to the fire, and she did not raise her voice.
    76	
    77	"You made us say it back to you. All of it. You made me walk a farm track in the dark with my hand on Brom's coat and count people by touch, and you said if a thing's worth teaching it's worth trusting." She pointed a stick at him. "So either you taught us right, and you can sleep, or you didn't, and you'd better say so now, because I've been doing it your way for five nights."
    78	
    79	Seln looked at her for some time.
    80	
    81	"That is not a fair argument," he said.
    82	
    83	"It's a perfectly fair argument. You just don't like where it comes out."
    84	
    85	"I've counted," said Brom, from the far side of the fire. "It's you. I counted it again."
    86	
    87	"There is also the principle," said Karis. "If the rota's to be set aside for judgment, I should want to know whose judgment, and on which nights. I can't keep a table that has *unless he would prefer otherwise* in its last column."
