editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:"It stays true. A house does not certify a tier and cannot uncertify one, and that line is on the form only so that the registry may know whom it is reading about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:25:She took the pen and wrote the day, and her name, with the long tail on it that she had been told at Fenmark was not correct. Then she looked at the third line, which had a rule printed under it and, at the rule's end, in the registry's small type, *the enrollee may leave this line blank*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:"It is," said Bracken. He had a second paper ready beside her leaf, and it ran to a full sheet. "An enrollee who holds standing under the research council does not only withdraw. She lapses, by heads, and the house is bound to read her the heads, so that nobody may say afterward that a thing was taken from her unawares. I drew it from the council's minute, and I believe it agrees with a sheet of your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:"I am going to say something that has no place in procedure," he said. "I shall not enter it. You may, if you keep books."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:249:He carried it down the corridor to the stove at the stair's head, which the porter kept in through the last week, for the damp. He opened the door and fed it the book a gathering at a time, and watched each one catch and go grey and fall in. It was the first record he had ever destroyed, and he thought that Karis would have understood it, and that Seln would not have needed to be told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:51:This one was looked up. The name on it stood in a district's roll of placed staff, with a posting against it and a date. The clerk who found it there would ordinarily have answered within the week: *not recalled by this office; no instruction; the house may fill the place.* He began to write exactly that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:He read it over. Every word was true. It was the last of its kind, and it was as good as any of them; a reader could have checked each line against a book and found it stood, and he would have learned nothing about a boy who had let a coat fall in front of twelve hundred people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:75:"That was an honest answer, and it is the whole of this case. Every leaf the house has filed went to the facts, and the petitioner has agreed the facts. What is left is a discretion. The registry may ask this question, the seat has held, if it chooses to, and I do not come here to say that it may not; I am bound by the seat's holding and I respect it." She paused. "I come to ask what the discretion is for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:123:"It was not written to be reached on purpose. The seat is asked to order a test in order to obtain a failure, so that the failure may open the chapter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Now I come back to the handle. These sections are for the testing of instruments, so that the registry may know which of them to trust, and there are two instruments before the seat. One is a frame, which was asked about this practitioner once and had no answer. The other is twenty-two officers of the registry. The house's counsel read their names to the seat at the preliminary, each with a grade and a house and a day: a provost's panel, a regional registrar's assessors, the tournament's, an evaluator of record who has sat across a trestle from him every week for two years. They watched, and wrote down what they saw, and signed it. Their notes agree with one another, and the petitioner has accepted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:177:Jent stood. He took off his spectacles, and let them fall on their cord, and stood for a moment with his hands at his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:215:Cael found that it was enough, and it surprised him. He had wanted a wall, a month ago, and had been given a blank on a form. But he had watched Gault hand up a leaf because it was there, and he had heard Karis spend fifty-one minutes on a room that could not be moved, and he thought he was beginning to learn how the honest ones did it. They did not do it to win. They did the thing that was theirs to do, to its full weight, and let it be entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:101:*The ninth, if it comes. It may not. I'm not to make it come; that's the first rule, and it's kept. He asked. Nobody put the asking in his mouth. He wrote it on the back of a chart in wing three and kept it till the seat had done with me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:251:Cael knew the walk. He had stood in front of it for a season on this floor, calling the weight aloud: every angle, every pace, the lath low and never thrown. The old read went out through his boots into sprung oak and came back with Ephram in it, early, as it always did. The weight was forward and a little left, the shoulders had not dropped, and the back heel was down. It was the front foot only, then, and the thing to do was wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:297:Five hands had gone up for this, and he had priced it. He looked down the length of the oak at a man leaning on a lath with the sun in his eyes, who had ruled a red line in the first month he knew him and had waited three years to be shown the far side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:299:He did not take the coat off. He let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:Karis had the table's end. Lira sat on the bench with her back to the wall and one boot up, and Brom was in the good chair from the dairy, which had somehow followed them up four flights months ago and never gone down. Seln came last, on the tenth bell, and shut the door behind him without a sound. He had his post satchel over his shoulder, and when he sat he did not hang it on the chair but set it on the floor between his feet, upright, and Cael heard the weight of what was in it meet the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:39:"With no date. Withrow underlined that twice in her own code, nine years apart. And three is part nine." He had it by heart, having heard Bracken read it, flat, on the night of the procedure. "They may restrict, freeze or hold anything that attaches to me: a record, a sponsorship, a travel paper, *any institutional relationship.* They've walked those fences already. They read Lira's file for completeness, and they wrote to Brom's family's estate, and they queried Karis's standing twice, and they sent two polite men to Denvash to ask about a shop's title."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:145:"*The practitioner is advised that associations of record with any person subject to an order under the fourteenth chapter may be noted upon the practitioner's file, at the registry's discretion, and may be considered upon any later review of standing or sponsorship. The practitioner may wish to regularize such associations before signature.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:175:"*Or to her order*," he said, and his voice was not quite steady. "I wrote that in a deposit book on the first day of the month. I have been thirteen years at that counter and I knew what I was writing." He put his lantern down on the top stair, carefully, and took the last pair of glasses off his forehead, and then there was nothing between his eyes and the lamp. They were wet, and he let them be wet. He stood on the landing with three pairs of spectacles in his hands and wept, briefly and without any noise, as a man does who has kept true records all his life and has just watched one do what this one had done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:307:"I told you at the trial that whatever you are, I was glad it was on my side of the floor," said Ephram. "I said some hard things on a tier in your first months here, and I've been glad every day since that I was wrong. I'd like to find out whether I'm still glad when you stop being careful with me. I think I will be. I'd like to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:53:"If it does not, the code says what follows. It said so before anybody here was born. The petitioner did not write the fourteenth chapter and does not ask the seat to add a word to it. He asks only whether the registry may put the question. That is a matter of discretion, and I have said so since the fourth sitting, in answer to the best question I was asked all year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:"It may," said Jent. "The seat held at the preliminary that the question was cognizable. Nothing the respondent has filed since has touched that holding, and nothing was meant to; her filings have been upon the facts, and the facts were hers before she filed. So I have nothing to answer. I have used ten minutes because the seat was generous with the hour, and I did not like to seem ungrateful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:61:Cael had sent the read after that man five times in the year and found nobody in the coat. He did not send it now, and he found that he no longer wanted to. He had spent months wishing Jent would turn out to be the arm, because an arm could be found out. But Jent was a hand, and a clean one. He had said exactly what he was asking for, in four sentences, before two hundred people, and every one of the four was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:75:"He is right that he does not need one. A discretion needs no precedent. But the seat may like to know, before it uses a tool, that nobody has ever picked it up by that end." The rule ticked again. "That is the house upon the remedy. Upon the merits, the house's close is the work of its advocate of record, who drew every submission this seat has received from it. The house renews its motion under the second paragraph of the hearing provision, and asks that she be heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:87:"The advocate of record for the respondent will be heard at the next sitting. She will be heard once, and within the hour. The petitioner may reply, if he is so advised, and not otherwise. The seat will then reserve." The level voice went on with no more weight on it than before. "So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:129:He found that he was not afraid of it. A month ago he would have been. He had stood at a rail in wing three and watched Ephram write on the back of a sheet, and had gone cold, because for two years the worst thing anybody on the hill could do to him was to ask. Now there was a date he could count to, and after it there would be either no band or a door, and in neither case anything left to guard from a friend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:43:"Every second day," said Karis, breathing hard. She had stopped beside it with her wedge. "I said so, and I was guessing. It may be every tenth, up here. It may be when somebody remembers." She touched the bare hook with one pale finger. "I wonder who decided. Somebody sat at a desk and decided that past this post a letter wasn't worth a horse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:*By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:85:"They run as far as my drawer." He nodded at the table. "There's been no keeper at the station these four years. They took the man and left me his brass, and a letter to say I was to mark for him until further instruction, and I've had no further instruction. So a sheet says *station*, and it means an old man at a turf fire a day short of it." He looked into the fire. "Nobody's complained. There's nobody past here to complain to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:195:Karis did not say so. She turned the leaves back with one finger. The entries were few, here at the end of every road, one a month perhaps, a shepherd's boy or a girl from a hold, all in a single careful hand, with every column filled. Then she turned forward again, to the ribbon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:"It stops," she said. "Four years ago. Here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:399:He sat on a rock above the water with a mule beside him, eating something out of a cloth, and he had two leather bags across the mule's back, flat, with long flaps. He looked at them across the stream without getting up. He had clearly been there some time, waiting for the water to fall, and was in no hurry about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:435:"Boxes," he said, to nobody, and shook his head. He rode down into the stream, which he had been waiting all morning to see fall, and went through it at a walk and up the far side, and he did not look back either. Nobody out here seemed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:125:The gelding had spent the night under the pentice and was no more pleased with the morning than Cael was. Lira backed him between the shafts, talking, and did up the buckles in the order she had been shown. Karis had lifted a corner of the canvas before she so much as washed, and counted three boxes, and let the corner fall and tied it down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:133:Then the healer had Ephram's wrist in one hand and his elbow in the other, and Brom had him round the chest. She said something short, and there was a second sound like the first, and Ephram made a noise that he afterward denied. She bound his arm across his chest and said the shoulder would want a month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:107:*Posting sought.* The district seat's records room was a man short. He had seen it for himself when he went there with a rubbing of a seal: an impression book four months behind, and a ledger of dies that nobody had balanced against the cutters' copies since the last officer left. He wrote *District records, Ostrand, or any records room that is short.* He did not write *out of monitoring*, for the form had no line for what a man wished to leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:129:They were at the head of the market street, the two of them, at the second bell on the last Fifth-day, where the cobbles began to fall toward the river. Below on the left were the four steps of the wool exchange, with people gathering on them already for a ruling that was hours off. Along a side street to the right, three corners down, a blue door stood in a plain stone front with a board beside it, and Brom and Lira were walking toward it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:"The rule is for water that covers a voice," he said. "And for water that things walk down to at night. That is a seep on a scree. It would not cover a cough, and nothing comes to it but us." He bent again. "A rule has its reasons written beside it so that one may tell when they are absent. I should stay where you are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:291:Nothing can be said of the room. It may be said that there was a table, and a lamp, since the work could not have been done without them. There was a clerk, and of the clerk it can be said only that the hand was practised, and that it had closed a great many files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:16:The second morning after the wall (day 51). Teague's crew went out at the previous dawn on the autumn road and is a week away. The five are thirteenth of thirteen with about ten marks, sleeping in the meadow. Nobody is hurt. Karis holds one word, *stillhound*, underlined.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:23:- **The ford walked at noon** (sill, slime, the gravel bar thirty by eight, pools that fall away, the near and far shingle). Brom finds his stone with a lip. Lira runs the bar. Seln lies in the grass for two hours. **The lore:** "Dusk. Never noon."; "River's their colour."; "It's because you die." The second man must read as having got *his hand between the jaws and his throat*, not as self-harm (Reader Standard).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-002.md:37:- Teague is absent: on the autumn road, then back and out again on hold slips. No meeting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:17:The switchback shelf above a valley with no name: the second day past the Line. Five people, a cart and a horse, three boxes and Seln's case under one canvas, two letters held for a courier who may not exist. Cael is eighteen, with nine held fragments, one anomaly, and a habit of counting watchers that has nothing left to count. "Nothing anywhere was deciding what he was, except him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:21:Deliver Arc 3's promise whole before anything complicates it: a country with roads and no paper, an institution (the board) that prices outcome and never tier, and a discipline (the hold) that turns four years of not-using into the only competence that pays. Teach the system hard. Turns and promises:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:26:- **Three contracts as short episodes.** The ferry escort (day 14; six crossings; two marks; standing where they can be seen, a job he has done before "at a higher price and for worse people"). The fence-line at the steading (days 22–24; Brom cuts six hours; Karis is asked not to light, does not ask why, and writes down the not-asking; "Cheap"). The wash (day 33; rope; Lira on the far bank, Brom on the near; three wheels; "You're not paper" / "I am"). The ferry broker may carry the hook-and-bar mark, as a plant only. Nothing about him is implied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:28:- **The Thornwater road (days 41–43), ≈4,000:** the carter at the fire ("Two carts went quiet on it last month"); the stretch at dusk after a wheel cost an hour; positions; the valley's noise; **Lira's hand up at a hole in the noise**; Seln without turning, "Don't declare anything." / "Walk. Same pace."; "I could clear it." / "No." / "I know. I'm saying it so I've said it."; Brom becoming exactly as large as his body and no larger; Karis deciding five times not to take out the notebook; the five items (keeps station at forty meters; makes no sound; tracks the buckle; won't cross the gravel fan; reacts to nothing because they gave it nothing); it stops a hundred meters short of the fence. No blow struck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:29:- **Thornwater:** the gate, the woman with the lantern, the bowman who doesn't point; "First crew I've hired that knew to shut up."; "Stillhound… You shut up. It went home."; "You're paper." / "Yes." / "Hm."; the slip marked; the grain loft; six days; the carter back on the fourth; Pike's copied line; the log with "Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:49:Day 49: thirteenth of thirteen, with eleven lines between them and Teague. "I'm free. First time." Something out here hunts Paths, and nobody has given them the reason. Teague has read them and been read. The ford clearance, forty marks, sits on the plank with HOLD and his name beside it, and his crew is going out on the autumn road for a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:22:- **The Ring (≈8,000–9,500 across two chapters), a retreat with a geometry.** The line is the objective. Nine counted on the white. "The road's cut." / "The grass. Where the cord went." Brom holds the lip: a hand's-height drop that robs a hound of drive, the haft, a man the pack cannot hear. Lira runs the rim's turned edge; the ankle goes a third time; the forearm taken and held; she cannot burst it off. **Brom's bolt through the eye at four meters.** Oryn's hands: "Nothing… Nothing in my hands." Cloth and knees. "Move. I can't fix this here. Move." Brom's string opens his fingers in the dark. The lane counted down, 160 to 60, the scrub moving at Lira's pace and never showing a shape. They are *careless* in here, because no prey has ever shouted. **Karis down at sixty**, hands on a throat, the jaws closing across her fists, the loudest sound on the ring. **Shadow, once, ten strides** (circle and Oryn only). The throw. Then *he stops holding*: Wind full, north off the lane; every head turns; then everything, one after another, so there is never a breath when he is not the loudest thing on the floor. Pressure for noise; Ember held out; Blade ringing with nothing to cut; Storm seeding the rut; Anchor off its hold and looking west. North to the rut, **east the wrong way** about a hundred and twenty meters, back west. Seln's "Back" from the stone and Lira's a breath after. The second lock with Compression at contact. Over the stone. **The bolt at two meters.** Anchor's perception sees Karis's points before they light: **the wall of ignition along the line's inside edge**, from her knees, voiceless. Seven break out north and south along the outside; two dead. Win-sentence and costs on the page. The opponent learned nothing it could use, because it had never needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:23:- **The hour's mending at the line by lantern:** "There. Back." (she does not mention the clock). The state, said because a thing not said is not a finding. **The order (protected, exact)** and the sling; "One arm. From here to the snow."; "The ankle's a tape and a season…"; Oryn flat on the road. Karis's written lines. Seln: "Nothing." Brom: "Hand." / "Morning. Wash it. I've nothing tonight." The log, including **the Book 9 plant line, exactly.** Close: "It worked in there." / "Yours." / "Yes." / "Later." / "Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:44:Day 100, the back room, minute twenty-one. A tenth fragment that does nothing in a fight; a woman who could not consent and has just been told she will be told; a covenant page in the loft; Lira slung for the season; Karis voiceless; a completion report about to go west with "Six went in" on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:9:- POV: Cael ≈35,400. **Seln cutaway ≈2,200** (night of day 112, after the council: the two slips laid side by side in his head; six years carrying a case for people he cannot see; why he wants a face; he may not open the case or decode anything). **Teague cutaway ≈2,400** (days 113–115: at Millrace, the five not back; the walk south to look; the rim's ground read in lantern light, the crossbows, "declaring"; holding the rim at dawn without being asked; no crew names).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:16:First light on the 111th at Thornwater: Oryn gone north, her mule with the crew. Lira is two weeks into the season's order. A completion report sits on a desk west of the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:27:- **The reading taught on the road:** "Well?" / "May I read you?" / "Yes…" Surface at ten: Tide *there*, the first thing that has ever been there at ten. A minute of the long one: banks, one channel, a current that runs *to* things. "You're built to arrive." / "Yes… That's what I am." "He asked twice and got one." / "That's the rate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:29:- **Day 114, the trap (≈9,000–10,500 across two chapters).** The crossing in Seln's order. **The bolt at forty meters**: "Ridge." Six hooded on the rim in road grey (two south, two east, one west, one north). **The Anchor course** closing the line behind them; Cael sees it with perception only. Bolts skip off the fused plate. **Lira runs *down***, and the unit follows. **Forty steps.** The chamber, ten strides square, its ceiling the floor; walls carved; at the middle "something cased… a cord," unread. **The doorway**: "They can shoot the bowl… They can't shoot the stair." **Oryn without hands**: lamp, eyes, two fingers; "Tape it. Leave the bolt."; "the bolt's the plug"; "I came south for exactly this. Enter that." Lira's mend reopened on the stair wall. **The stalemate, given room**: the square of grey going white and grey; a bolt sent down to stop at the thirty-first step; Brom grey and not worse; Karis with her back to the wall she would give a year to read. The design read aloud ("It thinks the floor did the same thing to all six of us."). **The order:** up alone with the one he was given. Oryn: "Ask first." / "I'm not reading anyone." / "I know. I'm saying the rule out loud so it's been said on this floor too." Lira: "Later." **The ascent, Shadow alone**, across the bowl unseen; the rim scanning for a shout that cannot exist. Anchor let off at ten meters as perception, then as a hand: **the knot found and released**, the course going slack like rigging. **Over the stone, declaring**: Wind, Storm seeding the rut, Compression at contact, Ember held out. He removes one man from the plan and does not fight him. The rim breaks as professionals break. The west one looks back for a second, hood up, nothing in it. Four crossbows left; no faces. "Back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:28:- **Day 80, Oryn**, introduced through her walk and then her hands: "Who's worst?"; the set ("That's the part you'll mind"); the mend in less than an hour (Lira: "Cold… No. It's going along."); she sits as a carter sets down a known load; "That's one."; "Hands on. That's the only way it works… And I can't do it to me."; Brom's wrist ("Then it should have closed in a month and hasn't, because you've pulled on it."); Karis: "Ember. And nothing. You're empty."; "Hm." at the lattice line. **The first failed surface reading** on Cael: she stops in the middle, looks at her hands, tries again, and her face does the instrument's thing. The protected exchange ends on "That's a *finding*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:29:- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:28:- **Day 96, the perimeter (≈4,000, measured set piece):** procedure said once (Lira the instrument a stride inside; Karis on the line with the cord; Brom marking; Seln ahead; Oryn at the stone; Cael writing counts). The bend in Karis's wrist; **iron at the third cord**; sixty-two cords and twenty strides; ten cords to the middle; "A circle is a made shape." said once as geometry and not followed. **The stake**: registry stamp, thirty-one, a boot's width on the line, "Five years in the weather. A winter either side."; "You drive them where you've measured to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:47:Day 96, the sun on the ridge and going: six people a hundred and forty meters inside a perfect circle, at the far lip of a den. The middle box is on Brom's back and the healer's hands don't work. The stone is two hundred meters west. One of them has told nobody that nothing happened to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-006.md:45:The sensation-language of listening, kept to sensation; Thornwater in the back of autumn (ice on the trough, the grain going up the ladder past where they sleep); the tables' daily rhythm; the bowman's manner and words; the birthday's warmth and humor; the shape of each finding scene; all the windows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:22:- **The bearing walk (116–118), on the page:** a road, then a track, then a carter's memory. Karis does not once say *if*. The country going into high autumn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:23:- **The Drowned Hall from the rise:** roofless, twelve strides square, a spring through the broken floor, a marsh exactly as wide as the Quiet. "Third." **The line** at the marsh's edge ("Gone"/"Back"); **stake thirty-three** (old, and no second); mud **heaved from below** along the water's edge ("It's never been out."); a bar at the hall's lip **bent from below**, read from a hundred and seventy meters. **Oryn's lore**: "Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:32:- **Lowmarch (129–131):** the fifth honest catalogue, from the landing. **The season line read** (protected); the ledger turned toward him; **no number**; "too small" (protected); the room's "Eleventh." / "Aye." **Teague's term** and "Noted." The healer's single look at Brom's shoulder. **The winter fence** given over ("Ours."). Karis takes the back room and pays without counting (ninety-six). **The inventory** (protected; the audio rule). **The last ferry**: nine pieces sorted aloud; **Hesk's straightedge** with the note (protected); the replies west (protected). **Oryn's letter by carter** the morning after (protected): "Six, by letter." "She'll say when." / "Spring." **The map**: four circles, the bearing, both edges laid along it, **touching the three paced centres for Cael exactly as for Karis**. "The sites were on an alignment. Not random." "Someone made this pattern." Four seconds. **The closing, exact.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:47:The bearing's country; the Hall's water and stone; the fight's moment-to-moment inside the taught rule; the marsh's two days; Teague's manner beside the fire; the walk's last autumn; the crest at dusk; the road home into snow; the inn by lamp; every window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:51:Book 8 Ch1: Lowmarch with the ice coming and the ferry's ropes taken in. Ninety-six in hand. A kit on a table. A map with a line going off its edge. A healer at Thornwater with a route and an arm to release. A captain's spring term. A fourth circle twice the size behind a fence of iron. And a question asked once at a table that nobody answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:53:- **Seasons:** late summer into the back of autumn, with first snow on the 129th. The mail ferry runs monthly: inbound on about the 60th and the 91st; outbound on the 102nd; the last run on the 130th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:34:- **Brom:** right shoulder bolted on the Stair and mended, reopened in the Hall and mended a second time ("Twice… not three times this season"). Then bound by Teague's healer of no Path and carried guarded. Carries the kit and the middle box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:39:- **Pike's board:** **nine completed lines** under the five names (the last three are the traverse, the Ring and the Stair). The Stair's price column reads *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The **season line** has been read aloud. **No line number entered**: "First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small." The five names stay **eleventh** on the wall, unchanged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:40:- **Teague:** "Spring. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." / "Noted." His crew (four plus an older healer of no Path) winters at Lowmarch. He has given the axe-head hold's long fence (forty, HOLD) to the five for the winter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:43:- **The Quieting, as known:** four sites on one alignment. The Fallow Ring, the Long Stair and the Drowned Hall have been entered. The fourth was paced from outside on the 123rd: **four hundred metres' radius**, ringed by a **cordon of iron stakes numbered thirty-four, with the stroke-and-bar mark, a pace apart**. Not entered, not named, its effect unknown, its central shape a standing grey thing nobody has a word for. "Someone made this pattern." was said once and not answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:59:| 4 | The Thornwater carter at the fire; the stretch at dusk; Lira's hand up at the hole in the noise; "Don't declare anything"; the grey shape at forty meters; the five items; it stops a hundred meters short of the fence (Ch1) | 41–43 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:60:| 5 | Thornwater's gate: "First crew I've hired that knew to shut up." "Stillhound." The six-day stay; Pike's copied line; the log, "Season's open" (Ch1) | 43–48 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:79:| 24 | The hour's mending at the line; the arm saved and the season's order; "It worked in there." / "Yours." / "Later." (Ch11) | 96 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:94:| 39 | Lowmarch in first snow: the season line and no number; Teague's term; the inventory; Hesk's straightedge; letters; Oryn's letter; the map; "Someone made this pattern."; the last lines (Ch24) | 129–131 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:141:| Cutaway | Words (share) | Placement (movement: words) | What the window may know | What it may never show |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:165:| Teague | To stay worth what he did, with his people counted first | Reads the Fallow slip twice and puts it back; puts the Stair slip back; "we'll come look" — and comes; holds the rim unasked; brings his healer three days along a bearing; asks "What are you" plainly; gives the five the winter fence; sets a spring term | 12 |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:206:| M1 | The Thornwater stretch at dusk: a mile with a grey shape at forty meters, nobody declaring | 3,500–4,000 | Tension set piece; no blow struck |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:222:**Injuries tracked forward (no silent healing):** Cael's calf (53 → reopened 78 and 96 → closed 99); ribs (78 → taped 81 → off 99 → the Hall's bill 119 → cold-aggravated 123 → quiet at 129). Lira's right ankle (53, 78, 96; **never mended**); her leg (broken 78, mended 80); her left forearm (96 → season order → reopened 114 → closed; slung at 131). Brom's forearm (53 → reopened 78 → mended 80); his hand (96 → mended 97); his shoulder (114 → mended; reopened 119 → mended twice → bound 120). Karis's palms (53, scars); empty (78); throat and voice (96 → mended 97, voice back by ~110). Seln: unmarked. Oryn: drained at every mending, priced aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:240:Callbacks confirmed in Book 8's closed prose: Oryn's letter and "Find me at Thornwater." / "Bring the map." (B8 Ch1, Ch24); "a tape and a season" (B8 Ch1); "Lie still when I say" (B8 Ch18, Ch24); Brom's "Twice… not three times this season" (B8 Ch6); "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it." (B8 Ch1, Ch5, Ch20); Hesk's "true to a hair" / "draw a line through it" / "Mind the shape" (B8 Ch1, Ch17, Ch18, Ch22, Ch24); "too small" (B8 Ch16, Ch17, Ch24); "Stop touching it." (B8 Ch7); "Not the Quiet." (B8 Ch7, Ch24); "He'll speak to you when you've cost him something" (B8 Ch7); Teague's spring term (B8 Ch1, Ch17); "Eleventh" (B8 Ch1, Ch12, Ch17); "the Bronze from Ardenmere" (B8 Ch7); ninety-six in hand (B8 Ch1); fifty-two sheets and forty-one kinds (B8 Ch9–11). Canon documents quote "Three points make a line. Four make an argument.", "Someone made this pattern.", the looms finding, "A circle is a made shape." and the stratum limit. Every other item below is a signature line or document of this book. **Keep them all exact.** Speaker attributions may be re-staged; the words may not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:259:- Pike's season line (Ch24): "*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*" — and the Stair's price column: *three hundred; taken; not paid*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:269:- **Oryn's letter (Ch24):** *Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. Spring: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.* — then: Six, by letter. (The architecture and series docs still quote "eleven days"; prose and Book 8 say **three weeks**. See C6.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:272:- Ch1 close: *Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:273:- Ch2, the first pole: *No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:274:- Ch3, the seal: *Seln did the shadow work tonight. I didn't. Out here there's a ledger and a ferry west once a month, and a defector's tradecraft in my hands would tell anyone who reads carefully where I learned it. The seal holds. Different country, same arithmetic.* — and *First honest number anyone's written about us in a year. It says four of five got hurt. It's right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:284:- Ch14, the birthday line: *Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, autumn, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:300:- Ch3: "It's because you die," — the steading's woman: "Four," … "Out of five. Two men went down to that ford this month and one came up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:304:- Ch7: Oryn: "That's a tape and a season. It'll set." — "Twenty days," … "Round. Two at a hold, near enough, and the road between, and the road's the part that doesn't move." — "I remember who I've had my hands on," … "That's the book. A healer who needs a page to know who's owed has stopped looking at them." — "In the city I healed Bronzes with bruises. Out here I set legs. I'm not going back and I'm not stopping. The route is the only thing I own." — "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it." — "When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly." — "Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken." / "Nine days." — The ford-keeper: "Then they go round," … "That's worth seventy to know before the first cart's in the gorge. I'd sooner pay it for this." — Teague: "You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five." / "Yes." / "The ford was good work. The bench was a cart." … "The board's going to have you and me on the same contract inside a season. Be good enough that I'm glad."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:307:- Ch10: Karis: "Sixty-two cords and twenty strides," … "Twelve hundred and fifty-six meters, and a piece." … "A circle is a made shape." — Lira: "like a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time." — Seln: "You drive them where you've measured to. Not where you've measured from." … "Five years in the weather. A winter either side." — Brom: "Skin." — Karis: "It costs nothing. There's nothing to spend." — Seln: "I've a knife and fifteen years. It was never most of what I do." — Oryn: "Nothing. Not nothing in him. Nothing in my hands." … "A healer's hands stop working there." — Cael: "It was dark before," … "It's dark now." — Karis: "None I own. And I own more than the registry prints." — Seln: "They live in here because nothing that walks in can fight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:308:- Ch11: Seln: "The road's cut." … "The grass. Where the cord went." — Oryn: "Move," … "I can't fix this here. Move." … "She keeps it." — **The order:** "A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone." … "One arm. From here to the snow." — "The ankle's a tape and a season. I've told you that before." — Close: "It worked in there." / "Yours." / "Yes," Cael said. / "Later." / "Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:315:- Ch18: Oryn: "No." … "You asked. That's the rule and you kept it. The answer's no. Next." — "You're asking me to break the route," / "I'm not asking. I'm telling you where we'll be." / "That's worse. That's a healer's sentence." — "I'm not going to be anybody's sixth." — **Terms:** "You're on the route now. Which means when I say lie still, you lie still, and when I say I can't help you, you believe me." — "Well?" / "May I read you?" / "Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it…" — "You're built to arrive." / "Yes," she said. "That's what I am." — Lira: "He asked twice and got one," / Brom: "That's the rate," — Seln: "Newer. This one's been re-surveyed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:316:- Ch19: Brom: "Ridge," — Seln: "They can shoot the bowl," … "They can't shoot the stair." — Oryn: "I can see what's wrong. I can't feel it and I can't touch it. Tape it. Leave the bolt." … "the bolt's the plug" … "I came south for exactly this. Enter that." — Oryn: "Ask first," — Lira: "Later." / "Later." — Oryn at the stone: "There. Back," … "The clock started again." — "It held a stair. It's not going to hold anything else this season." — Seln: "No faces." — Teague: "You walked out of the Quiet declaring," / "Yes." … "Then you're not on the board anymore. You're something the board doesn't have a column for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:317:- Ch20: Teague: "We'll hold it till you're up." / "Noted" — Oryn: "What are they" / Karis: "Lines," — Karis: "He tried to drive it. Twice, by the head. It wouldn't go. Nothing goes into this." — Seln: "Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another." … "I don't know what this is in, and I'm not going to say." — Karis: "Forty-one," … "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred meters and a stride, to the stride." … "The floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size," … "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:318:- Ch21: Oryn: "Big. Under. Comes up. Nobody's seen it whole." — Seln: "It's blind. It reads weight on the floor. It strikes where the weight is. And it strikes what *stays*." — Karis: "It reads that as the heaviest thing on the floor," / Cael: "It reads that as the thing that *stays*," — Lira: "We're going in without Paths to look at walls with a thing under the floor." / "Yes," Karis said. / "Just checking that's the plan." — Seln: "It's blind. It reads the floor. Get *off* the floor." — "You're copying," / "I'm not getting another chance," / "No, I can see that you're not," — *A thing that hunts what stays commits to the thing that stays hardest.* — "Brom," Cael said. "Wall. Your weight. Now." — Oryn: "Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to" — "You went in the water." / "You went in for Brom." / "Brom's on my route." … "So are you." … "Lie still. I want to read you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:321:- Ch24: Pike: "Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small." — the carters: "Eleventh," / "Aye," — Teague: "Spring. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." / "Noted," — Pike: "Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the winter road," / Brom: "Ours," — Lira: "She'll say when," / Brom: "Spring," — *The sites were on an alignment. Not random.* — Karis: "Someone made this pattern." (said once; nobody answers) — **Closing, exact:** "I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one." / Lira, one-armed, feeding the fire: "Then we follow it." / Brom: "Spring." / Karis, writing: "Noted." / Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the snow on the ferry landing with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home. / Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:327:| Reserved truth | This book may | This book may NOT |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:357:- **C8 · The Tide stall.** The bible (PROVISIONAL) defines a combat stall that Oryn "has done three times in her life." Neither Book 7 nor Book 8 shows it. **Recommend:** at most one mention, in Oryn's teaching in M3 as a limit she names and hates. Never used, since she is never made to fight. Owner may prefer omission.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:358:- **C9 · Lira's ankle.** The architecture checkpoint says the ankle was "mended once (Ch6)." The prose mended the tibia; the ankle is never mended in Book 7 ("a tape and a season"), and Book 8 Ch1 mends it. **Prose governs.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:360:- **C11 · Small counts.** Ch17 has Pike turning the ledger "which he had done once before" (it was twice: Ch2, Ch8). Ch24 has "twice in a season" (it is the fourth). Ch22 has "no captain eleven lines up" when Teague is ten up at eleventh. The retelling counts honestly. Convention: "N up" counts positions above; "N between" counts lines between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:379:- Drafting may begin on the defaults. Ask the owner to rule on **C1** before Movement 1 is drafted, since it touches its opening chapters. C2, C3 and C8 can be ruled on before M3 and M6.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md:381:- Book 8's DECISIONS file lists the facts Book 8 must not contradict. Every one is reproduced in this map's §1 ending, and none may move.

