   176	
   177	He came to the eighteenth stone a little after it was light.
   178	
   179	There was a crossroads beyond it. A lane ran north to a farm and another south toward the river, and in the angle of them lay a piece of rough common with a thorn brake along one side, and a stream that went under the south lane through a pipe of fired clay. He had passed the place twice in two years, on the house's errands, and had marked it each time for no reason he would have admitted.
   180	
   181	He walked the ground first, as he did with any ground.
   182	
   183	The brake would hide a fire from the road until a traveller was nearly on it, and that was to the good. The common drained, and the grass was cropped; sheep had been on it, and not lately. He went to the stream and stood on its bank, and then he turned his back to the water and began to pace.
   184	
   185	Forty paces. It was his own rule, and he had given it in an orchard with the reasons: water covers a voice at forty paces and a foot at ten, and things walk to water at night. He had told them it was to be forty paces of the shortest person present, and the researcher had written in her notebook, quite seriously, *of whose?*
   186	
   187	So he shortened his stride to hers. He had watched her cross a court often enough to know it. It brought him to a level place under the brake's lee, with a flat stone in it, and he looked at that and found nothing wrong.
   188	
   189	He set the satchel down against the stone, and slept two hours with his back to it, sitting, as he had slept in coaches. When he woke, the sun had come round, and he began on the wood.
   190	
   191	It took him the rest of the day, and that surprised him. He had supposed that a man gathered sticks for an hour and had done. He had not built a fire out of doors since he was a boy on a quay, and in nineteen postings every hearth he sat at had been lit by somebody whose work it was. He went along the brake and found that most of what lay under it was rotten, and that what was sound was green. He went up the north lane as far as a fallen ash and broke off what he could with his hands and his boot. He carried it back in his arms, four journeys, and each time he came round the thorn he looked at the heap and thought it small.
   192	
   193	There would be five of them, and one fire.
   194	
   195	He kept arriving at that as he worked. They would have come from first light with a loaded cart, and a ford, and a horse none of them knew. The boy would have an arm he could not close and two days of bills come due at once, and the girl would have talked to the horse the whole way and would be hoarse. They would come round that thorn at dusk, tired through, and cold, since people are always cold at the end of the first day.
   196	
   197	He had spent his working life at the edge of other people's tables. He had been fed at them, and had liked a great many of the people he sat among, and had written every one of them down afterward, in the old pattern, and sent it up. He had never once got anywhere first, and made it ready, for people who would be glad to find it so.
   198	
   199	He broke the ash across his knee and laid it small to large, as a quay-man had shown him forty years ago. He was absurdly careful about it.
   200	
   201	There was one thing left over from the old life, and it came at its hour.
   202	
   203	On a Seventh-day he wrote a return. He had not written this week's; he had been at a stove. But the pattern was fifteen years deep and did not know that it had been retired, and while his hands laid sticks it composed the thing by itself, in the voice he kept for the audit.
   204	
   205	He took a pencil and a leaf from his coat. He wrote it down, to be rid of it.
   206	
   207	*Subject withdrew from the house on the Seventh-day, by the registrar's form, in good standing. Subject left the house at dusk with three associates and a cart. Subject's floor work is in the house's book for the Sixth-day, which this officer attended. Subject's associations unchanged. Nothing further to report.*
   208	
   209	He read it over. Every word was true. It was the last of its kind, and it was as good as any of them; a reader could have checked each line against a book and found it stood, and he would have learned nothing about a boy who had let a coat fall in front of twelve hundred people.
   210	
   211	*Subject's associations unchanged.* He looked at that line for some time. Then he took the pencil and added one word to the end of it, which no return of his had ever carried.
   212	
   213	*Five.*
   214	
   215	He did not fold it in the old way. There was no outside fold to put a reference on, and no bag, and no drawer at the end of any road he meant to walk. He twisted the leaf into a spill instead, and put it under the smallest sticks, where the heap wanted something dry.
   216	
   217	The light went slowly. He had water in the can and the can on its stone, and he had walked forty of somebody else's paces twice more, to be sure.
