   195	Karis did not say so. She turned the leaves back with one finger. The entries were few, here at the end of every road, one a month perhaps, a shepherd's boy or a girl from a hold, all in a single careful hand, with every column filled. Then she turned forward again, to the ribbon.
   196	
   197	"It stops," she said. "Four years ago. Here."
   198	
   199	The last line had a day on it. It had a name, a girl's, and an age, which was fourteen.
   200	
   201	After the age there was nothing. The reading was blank, and the narrow column, and *Entered*. The keeper's hand had come as far as the fourth column and had not gone into it, and there was no blot, and no stroke through the line. Below it the leaf was ruled and empty to its foot.
   202	
   203	"He was called away in the middle of it," said Brom.
   204	
   205	"Or she was." Lira was looking at the name. "Or he had his letter that morning, and thought, what's the use."
   206	
