   235	In the morning each of them crossed the sill.
   236	
   237	The track ran between the house and the frame-house, and a sill of stone was set in the ground there from wall to wall, with a socket at either end. There had been a bar across it once, and a keeper to lift the bar. It was the last place on that road where an officer could have put out his hand for a paper.
