    96	
    97	His clerk came for the tray at the usual hour. The man had been nine years in the outer office, and he took up the day's paper against his chest and ran his eye down the top leaf, as he always did, and stopped. He turned it over to see whether the writing had gone on the back.
    98	
    99	"There's no annotation on this one, sir."
   100	
   101	"No."
   102	
   103	"It's the Halcenvane matter." The clerk hesitated. In nine years he had not carried a summary down that stair without something in its margin. "Shall I enter it as *no observation*?"
   104	
